//@version=6
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v7.2 · ICT LIQUIDITY + PD ARRAY TRADE ENGINE      (from v7.1, file 21)
// Companion documents: AUDIT-v7.1-FORENSIC.md (the audit that produced this file)
// and AUDIT-v7.2-ICT.md (what changed, why, and the re-audit).
//
// v7.2 is a PURE FORENSIC FIX PASS. No module was added, none removed, no input
// added and none removed; one input (needSweep) was retitled and re-defaulted.
// Eight defects fixed, P0 first:
//   BUG-01 P0 request.security to a timeframe BELOW the chart leaked future data
//              through lookahead_on. f_tfUp clamps every requested tf up.
//   BUG-02 P0 the confluence (S/D) producer inherited the most recent raid on its
//              side with no spatial relation to the zone it traded. Containment
//              now required, at the one site both sides pass through.
//   BUG-03 P1 zone tier 1 was partly bought with the GLOBAL chart OTE.
//   BUG-04 P1 retBeforeFail was true on the very bar that read it, so BREAKER
//              classification was missing one of its three requirements.
//   BUG-05 P1 an unowned displacement promoted a grade-C raid to grade B, which
//              is the grade-A rung's own requirement.
//   BUG-06 P1 the elite trap's advertised A+ eligibility was unreachable at the
//              default, and inverted when oteNeedAp was switched off.
//   BUG-07 P1 the trap's "the broken level must be tracked liquidity" never
//              compared the broken pool with the level that armed the trap.
//   BUG-10 P3 two alertconditions duplicated "Any LONG" / "Any SHORT".
//
// V7.1 · ENTRY-ACCURACY PASS. Four surgical changes, no architecture touched:
//   1 · POI BINDING IS CONTAINMENT (§7 / §40) — the array must hold the raid
//       extreme, not sit two ATR from it; it must not be older than poiMaxAge;
//       and the displacement must genuinely leave it (POI_LEAVE_ATR 0 → 0.10).
//   2 · DISPLACEMENT HAS FOUR REQUIREMENTS (§2) — its own body input
//       (dispBodyAtr, default 1.3 × ATR), a close beyond the MSS level, a close
//       outside the POI, and a close that HOLDS (dispCloseFrac); a candle that
//       hands the expansion back through the MSS level kills the setup.
//   3 · THE TRAP ENGINE CAN REACH TIER 1 (§15 / §16) — an ELITE trap (all five
//       ingredients) gets a tier-1 POI, is A+ eligible, and is admitted in
//       every entry mode including "Narrative only".
//   4 · LATE ENTRY + OTE ARE GRADED (§10 / §11 / §48) — A+ only on the first
//       or second touch of the execution gap; OTE returns a BAND CLASS
//       (62-70 % prime = full points + A+ · 70-79 % deep = half points, A max
//       · > 79 % = one point, never A+).
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// 1 · CRT AND TURTLE SOUP ARE GONE, IN FULL
// Removed: fifteen inputs, the Crt and Tbs UDTs and both five-stage lifecycle
// machines (each instantiated twice), the four turtle-soup extreme series, the
// two cooldown pairs, f_ctt_crtCand / f_ctt_tbsCand / f_ctt_htfCand, the HTF
// CRT/TBS request.security, the entire CTT marking layer (Ctt UDT, f_cttTxt,
// f_cttMark inlined four times, f_cttLvl, f_ctt_chart, f_ctt_htfMark), the
// PR_CRT and PR_TBS producer branches in f_prod, their consumption branches,
// their dashboard row and five alerts. Verified absent: no identifier
// containing crt / tbs / ctt / turtle survives outside prose.
//
// 2 · WHAT REPLACED THEM — CAUSALITY, NOT ANOTHER PATTERN
// The brief's rule is that confluence may not stand in for causality. The
// single worst violation in v6.4 was not CRT: it was f_gradeZone awarding a
// TIER 1 POI from two GLOBAL flags — "a raid exists somewhere on this side"
// and "a shift happened within ten bars" — neither of which had to be near the
// zone or in the right order. Tier 1 is what the grade ladder, the A+ rung and
// the minTier gate are all built on, so that one substitution mis-priced every
// decision downstream of it. It is now causal and the evidence is recorded on
// the zone (srcPoolId / srcRaidBar / srcMssBar / srcDispBar).
//
// 3 · ICT COMPONENTS ADDED
//   §5  BOS / CHoCH / MSS / CISD separated. v6.4 had mssUp = chochUp or cisdUp,
//       which made every delivery flip a structure shift. A CISD is now an MSS
//       only when it carries displacement (mssNeedDisp).
//   §6  HTF external structure published from the HTF's own pivots (hStrDir),
//       so the hierarchy is visible and an LTF shift cannot masquerade as it.
//   §7  HTF swing extremes registered as PK_HTF liquidity; target selection is
//       a RANKING (type, confluence, respect, distance) instead of "nearest".
//   §10 Premium / discount is a REVERSAL PRECONDITION, not a score term only.
//   §11 OTE measured on the setup's OWN impulse leg (raid extreme → displacement
//       extreme), not on the chart's global dealing range. A+ requires it.
//   §14 Order blocks: the last opposing candle of a displacement leg that also
//       broke structure and left an FVG. Never "every opposite candle".
//   §13 IFVG separated from BREAKER and MITIGATION by WHAT failed.
//   §17 BPR: an FVG opening inside a live opposing FVG re-tags the overlap.
//   §22 Trendline liquidity: confirmed anchors, correct slope, minimum touch
//       count, and it enters the registry only when SWEPT — so a trendline
//       break can never by itself produce anything.
//   §36 Market regime (TREND / RANGE / EXPANSION / CHOP) from price action.
//   §38 Inducement: a minor pool taken before the setup's own external raid.
//   §39 AMD / Judas as session CONTEXT, attached as an event descriptor.
//   §40 The POI stage ranks the PD ARRAY, not just the tier.
//   §48 PREMATURE / VALID / LATE. The clock starts at the FIRST touch of the
//       execution FVG; a rejection arriving later is refused outright.
//
// 4 · WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY *NOT* ADDED (§46 — do not overfit)
// No new oscillator, no candlestick-pattern library, and no second gate built
// on evidence an existing gate already reads. The regime classifier is
// reported and scored but adds NO gate, because congestion — the same
// evidence — is already wired as one.
//
// 5 · NON-REPAINTING
// Unchanged in kind, and unchanged in COUNT: six request.security call sites
// (f_bias, inlined at five timeframes; HTF context; PDH/PDL; PWH/PWL; HTF
// structure; HTF FVG POI) = ten instances, exactly as v6.4 had — the HTF
// structure feed took over the slot the CRT/TBS pair vacated. Every one reads
// an expression indexed [1] INSIDE the request with lookahead_on, which is the
// only combination that is both leak-free and stable. The new HTF structure
// feed follows the same construction and its pivots are ta.pivot*(2,2), so a
// level is visible only after the HTF candle that confirmed it has closed.
// Every state latch remains gated on `confirmed`.
//
// 6 · BUDGET (measured, same model the v6.4 audit calibrated)
// v6.4 = 31 873 source tokens ≈ 99 467 compiled of 100 256 — 789 spare.
// v7.1 — see AUDIT-v7.1-ICT.md for the measured budget.
// v7.0 = 31 555 source tokens ≈ 98 380 compiled — about 1 880 spare, with the
// whole ICT layer added. Main body 1 863 of the 2 034 the lineage is known to
// accept. If TradingView still returns CE10117, the cut order is: showTL off
// costs nothing but the trendline engine (~620 compiled); then MODULE 8B's
// liquidity marking (≈ 3 960); then the debug panel (≈ 3 680).
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Liquidity + Supply/Demand + Price Action Setups [MTF] — v5 "CAUSAL TRADE ENGINE"
//
// Built from v4. v4 introduced the VISUAL → CONFLUENCE → TRADE ENGINE → SIGNAL
// split and a per-direction setup state machine. v4's machine was still able to
// walk its whole narrative inside ONE candle, could claim a liquidity raid that
// happened BEFORE the setup existed, never checked that the raid actually hit
// the pool the setup was hunting, and scored itself from GLOBAL "something
// happened recently" flags that belonged to no setup at all.
//
// v5 makes the narrative OWNED and CHRONOLOGICAL, and adds a hard-gate layer
// that a high score can never buy its way past:
//
//     CONTEXT → TARGET → RAID → POI → MSS → DISPLACEMENT → FVG → RETEST
//         ↓        ↓       ↓      ↓      ↓         ↓          ↓       ↓
//      ctxBar < tgtBar < raidBar ≤ poiBar < mssBar < dispBar ≤ fvgBar < retestBar
//
// ── v5 ARCHITECTURE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  V5-1  EVENT OWNERSHIP. Every stage stamps its own bar AND the identity of
//        the object that produced it (targetPoolId, raidPoolId, poiId, mssLvl,
//        fvgBar). Nothing global can satisfy a stage any more.
//  V5-2  CHRONOLOGY IS A HARD GATE. Each transition compares bars, so the
//        machine can advance at most one stage per bar except the two pairs
//        that are physically simultaneous (raid candle = POI tap candle,
//        displacement candle = 3rd candle of its own FVG).
//  V5-3  TARGET ↔ RAID IDENTITY. The setup locks a target POOL ID at state 2
//        and state 3 only fires when THAT pool is raided. A different pool at a
//        similar price does not satisfy the setup.
//  V5-4  NO SAME-BAR RE-ARM. Any invalidation stamps resetBar; a fresh setup
//        cannot be armed on the candle that killed the previous one.
//  V5-5  RAID QUALITY GRADING (A/B/C) from penetration depth, close-back
//        strength and post-raid reaction — a 1-tick poke is no longer "a raid".
//  V5-6  POI TIER IS DYNAMIC. Tiers are re-graded every bar from mitigation,
//        HTF agreement and structure; a zone can fall T1 → T2 → T3 → dead.
//  V5-7  HARD GATES vs SOFT SCORE. Hard gates: narrative integrity, chronology,
//        ownership, RR floor, opposing-liquidity room, dedupe, tier, session /
//        volatility. Soft score only ranks what already passed.
//  V5-8  RR + OPPOSING LIQUIDITY are computed BEFORE the signal is emitted, so
//        a trade with no room to its first opposing pool is blocked, not drawn.
//  V5-9  GRADE = A+ / A / B, each with its own RR floor and its own evidence
//        requirements. A+ needs the complete owned narrative, a grade-A raid,
//        a tier-1 POI and RR ≥ the A+ floor. Score alone can never make an A+.
//  V5-10 HTF IS NON-REPAINTING BY CONSTRUCTION. Every request.security now
//        reads the LAST CLOSED HTF bar (expr[1] + lookahead_on), which is the
//        only combination that is both leak-free and stable in realtime.
//  V5-11 DEDUPE: one pool → one execution, one POI → one execution, one MSS →
//        one execution, plus the per-direction cooldown v4 already had.
//  V5-12 SESSION NARRATIVE: Asia→London and London→NY raids are recognised as
//        the liquidity story they are and scored as context, not as a filter.
//
// ── v4 DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED HERE (detail in AUDIT-v5.md) ────────────────
//  V4-BUG-1  SAME-BAR STATE CASCADE. f_setupAdvance ran the stage blocks as a
//            straight sequence of independent `if s.st == ST_x` tests inside
//            ONE call, and every stage's trigger could be true on the same
//            candle: HTF context is a standing condition, a liquidity target is
//            a standing condition, the raid/POI/MSS/displacement can all be the
//            same candle, and only state 7→8 had a `bar_index > s.fvgBar`
//            guard. A single candle could therefore walk 0→7. v5 stamps a bar
//            per stage and compares them (V5-2).
//  V4-BUG-2  RETROACTIVE RAID CLAIM. f_findRaid returned any pool raided within
//            `sweepLook` bars with `bound == 0`, and the setup was armed by
//            htfCtx on a LATER bar, so a setup born at bar 103 legally claimed
//            the raid from bar 100. v5 requires raidBar > tgtBar > ctxBar
//            ≥ bornBar.
//  V4-BUG-3  TARGET AND RAID WERE UNRELATED. State 1→2 stored `tgtPx` from the
//            nearest pool but state 2→3 called f_findRaid, which returns the
//            FRESHEST raid on that side — any pool. Target = EQL at 1.0850 and
//            raid = an unrelated swing low at 1.0910 satisfied the same setup.
//            v5 locks the pool id (V5-3).
//  V4-BUG-4  SAME-BAR RE-ARM. Every invalidation branch called f_setupReset,
//            and the very next block in the SAME call was `if s.st == ST_NONE`
//            → re-arm. A setup could die and be reborn on one candle, which
//            also reset its life clock forever.
//  V4-BUG-5  GLOBAL EVENT CONTAMINATION IN THE SCORE. f_score read
//            recentMssUp / recentDispUp / cisdUpBar / raidExtLo / sslStrong —
//            all global "did this happen in the last N bars" flags. Events from
//            a dead setup, or from the opposite side of the chart, paid the
//            current one. v5 scores setup-owned fields only.
//  V4-BUG-6  MSS COULD PRE-DATE THE RAID. State 4→5 accepted `chochUp or
//            cisdUp` with no comparison to s.liqBar, and because the whole
//            chain could run in one call the MSS was often the raid candle
//            itself. v5 requires mssBar > raidBar and a level that formed near
//            the raid, at least MSS_MIN_SEP_ATR away from the raid extreme.
//  V4-BUG-7  DISPLACEMENT WAS JUST A BIG CANDLE. State 5→6 accepted any
//            dispUpBar within dispWin, including the MSS candle itself. v5
//            requires dispBar > mssBar, a close beyond the MSS level, and a
//            close outside the POI (price actually left the origin).
//  V4-BUG-8  FVG UNRELATED TO THE DISPLACEMENT. State 6→7 accepted any bullFVG
//            within `fvgWin` (default 10) bars of the displacement. A gap ten
//            candles later, from a different leg, was locked as "the
//            displacement's FVG". v5 requires the displacement candle to be one
//            of the three candles that form the gap (bar_index - dispBar ≤ 2).
//  V4-BUG-9  HTF DATA WAS THE DEVELOPING HTF BAR. v4 removed v2's [1] from
//            every lookahead_off security call (its BUG-15). lookahead_off does
//            NOT mean "last closed bar" — it means "no future data", and the
//            forming HTF bar is not future data, so f_htfCtx / f_htfPoi /
//            f_crtTbsHtf / f_bias all read a bar that was still being built and
//            changed as it built. Worse, `newHtfBar` fires on the FIRST chart
//            bar of a new HTF candle, so the HTF CRT pattern was evaluated on
//            an HTF candle roughly one chart bar old. v5 uses expr[1] +
//            lookahead_on = exactly the last CLOSED HTF bar (V5-10).
//  V4-BUG-10 A RAID WAS A RAID AT ANY DEPTH. f_poolScan only needed `high >
//            p.px and close < p.px`, so a one-tick poke graded the same as a
//            0.6·ATR sweep with a full close-back. v5 needs raidMinAtr of
//            penetration to register at all, and grades A/B/C above that.
//  V4-BUG-11 POI TIER WAS FROZEN AT CREATION. f_gradeZone ran once, in
//            f_newZone. A T1 demand zone stayed T1 after the HTF flipped
//            bearish, after external structure broke down and after the zone
//            was 90 % mitigated. v5 re-grades every zone every confirmed bar.
//  V4-BUG-12 NO RR OR OPPOSING-LIQUIDITY GATE. MODULE 16 computed SL and TP
//            AFTER MODULE 15 had already emitted, so a signal whose structural
//            stop was 3·ATR wide with an un-raided pool 0.4·ATR overhead still
//            printed, drew a TP ladder and fired its alert. v5 computes risk
//            first and gates on it (V5-8).
//  V4-BUG-13 A+ WAS TWO CONDITIONS. `aPlusLong = prodL <= PR_MACH and tierL ==
//            1` — no RR, no raid quality, no chronology check, and tierL came
//            from the frozen creation tier of BUG-11.
//  V4-BUG-14 DEDUPE WAS COOLDOWN ONLY. After masterCd bars the same pool, the
//            same POI and the same MSS could produce a second and third entry,
//            because pool.bound was set but never consulted on re-entry and the
//            zone was only marked spent for the S/D producer.
//  V4-BUG-15 CONFLUENCE PRODUCERS HAD NO CHRONOLOGY. confGateLong was
//            `sslInPlay and (recentMssUp or mssUp) and recentDispUp` — three
//            independent global flags in any order, so "displacement, then MSS,
//            then an old sweep still in play" passed.
//  V4-BUG-16 REVERSALS WERE INVISIBLE. htfCtxLong was an OR chain including
//            `htfPd <= 0.5`, so a counter-trend long in a bearish HTF was
//            classified identically to a continuation long. v5 classifies the
//            setup as REVERSAL and demands grade-A raid + T1 POI + CHoCH.
//
// ── v5.1 · PHASE A — THE QUALITY LAYER WAS INERT AT DEFAULTS ───────────────
// An audit of v5 found that most of the "hard gates a high score can never buy
// past" evaluated TRUE unconditionally with the shipped inputs. At defaults,
// emission reduced to: a producer fired + POI tier ≤ 2 + cooldown + dedupe.
// Phase A restores the five gates that v5 claimed to have:
//  A1  RR WAS CIRCULAR. f_tp seeded t2 = entry + rrMult*risk and MODULE 15 then
//      computed RR as (t2-entry)/risk — i.e. rrMult, exactly. Defaults
//      rrMult 2.0 ≥ rrAplus 2.0 ≥ rrA ≥ rrB, so every RR floor passed on every
//      bar. In "Next level" mode f_tp never wrote t2 at all, making RR a literal
//      constant. TP2 is now the nearest opposing wall / external pool / internal
//      pool that is at least the grade-B floor away; when nothing qualifies the
//      new hTgt gate blocks the trade instead of inventing a target. TP1 is
//      capped to TP2 (v5 raised TP2 to TP1, so a 5R TP1 pool bought an A+ RR)
//      and tpClamp now clamps all three rungs, not only TP3.
//  A2  f_grade OPENED AT GR_B UNCONDITIONALLY, so a tier-3, un-graded-raid,
//      no-narrative candidate graded B and cleared the default minGrade = "B".
//      Grade B now requires a graded raid, the B RR floor and a real target;
//      below that the grade is GR_NONE and the trade does not exist.
//  A3  REVERSAL WAS 1 OF THE 4 PROMISED CONDITIONS (`not rev or mssCh`), and
//      failing it only downgraded into the grade that trades by default. It is
//      a hard block now. Separately, `not needHtfAlign or ...` made htfCtx
//      tautological, which bypassed allowRev AND the premium/discount-extreme
//      requirement; allowRev = OFF is enforced at the arming stage.
//  A4  THE ATR FALLBACK STOP BYPASSED THE RISK CLAMP. When every structural
//      level was rejected FOR BEING WIDER than maxRiskAtr, f_sl returned
//      entry ± atrStopMult*atr unchecked — a stop inside the level that
//      invalidates the thesis. f_sl reports slOk and MODULE 15 gates on it.
//  A5  OPPOSING LIQUIDITY WAS DOUBLE-COUNTED. rr = math.min(rrRaw, room) meant
//      the documented soft mode (oppLiqHard = OFF) hard-blocked anyway whenever
//      oppLiqMult ≤ rrB, and reported it as an RR failure. RR is pure now; room
//      is a gate (hard mode) or a one-step grade downgrade (soft mode).
//      "No opposing liquidity" is na, not the 99.0 sentinel v5 printed.
//  A6  RR floors and the risk clamp are normalised (rrAPn/rrAn/rrBn,
//      minRiskN/maxRiskN) so an inverted input cannot make A+ easier than A or
//      silently force every trade onto the ATR fallback, and every RR
//      comparison goes through f_geq to survive float cancellation.
//
// ── v5.2 · PHASE B — REGISTRY IDENTITY + RISK / LIFECYCLE CONSISTENCY ──────
//  B1  ENGINEERED / EXTERNAL POOLS COULD NOT REGISTER. f_poolPush refused any
//      pool within eqTol·ATR of a live same-side pool — and every EQH/EQL (the
//      mean of two pivots whose first pivot already registered as a SWING
//      pool ≤ 0.075·ATR away) and nearly every EXT pivot (the same price as
//      its earlier internal pivot) is exactly that. The registry therefore
//      almost never held PK_EQ or PK_EXT pools: raidEq*/raidExt* rarely fired,
//      the CRT/TBS location filter lost two of its legs, the liquidity score
//      paid 3 (swing) instead of 6–8, and the TP ladder's external scope was
//      empty, so hTgt blocked trades that had real external targets. A
//      duplicate that outranks a live un-raided SWING pool now UPGRADES that
//      pool in place (kind, tag, px, +touch) — id, binding, bornBar and touch
//      history are preserved, so nothing a setup owns is invalidated.
//  B2  DOUBLE STOP PADDING. CRT / TBS / TL / PA / HTF producers handed f_sl a
//      stop already padded by SL_PAD_ATR and f_sl padded every candidate
//      again — every confluence stop was 2× padded (the machine's raw liqExt
//      was correct). Producers now hand RAW levels; f_sl owns the pad.
//  B3  A TRANSIENT BLOCK NO LONGER DESTROYS THE NARRATIVE. A machine setup
//      that reached state 8 but was refused by cooldown / session / RR / room /
//      target / grade / arbitration died unconsumed on the next bar's state-9
//      cleanup. It now returns to state 7 and may retest again inside
//      retestWin. PERMANENT verdicts stay terminal: failed narrative integrity
//      and dedupe (this pool / POI / MSS already traded) still kill it.
//  B4  POST-RAID RECLAIM IS A BREAK. f_poolScan marked a post-raid close back
//      through the level PS_DONE without stamping brkBar, so the pool-break
//      flags missed it and the machine kept hunting an MSS off a failed sweep
//      until the deeper close-beyond-the-WICK test. Every close-beyond path
//      stamps brkBar now, and the machine invalidates on it.
//  B5  f_narrOk demanded only mssBar ≥ poiBar; the machine can never produce
//      the equality, so the independent gate said less than the machine does.
//      Also renamed two dashboard locals that shadowed the global freshS.
//
// ── v5.3 · PHASE C — LIVE TIER AT THE GRADE + HONEST DOCS ──────────────────
//  C1  THE GRADE READ A FROZEN POI TIER. s.poiTier was stamped once at the
//      stage-4 POI tap and never refreshed, while f_regrade decays every zone
//      every confirmed bar (V5-6). Up to retestWin bars later the entry was
//      graded — and hTier gated — on the tier the zone HAD, not the tier it
//      HAS: a T1 that fell to T2 (HTF flipped / structure flipped / 50 %
//      eaten) still bought an A+, which §6 of the audit explicitly promised
//      it could not. The machine now refreshes s.poiTier from the live zone
//      on every confirmed bar (na-safe f_zoneTier), so the grade, the hTier
//      gate and the debug panel all read the re-graded tier. The existing
//      f_zoneDead invalidation still kills the setup outright when the live
//      tier falls below minTier. HTF FVG POIs have no re-grade engine and
//      keep their fixed tier 1 — stated below as a known limit.
//  C2  LEGACY PRODUCERS WERE SILENTLY DEAD AT DEFAULTS. The trendline and
//      price-action producers carry no POI, so their tier is 3 — and the
//      default minTier = 2 hard-blocks tier 3, so "All engines (legacy)"
//      added producers that could never emit and nothing said so. Behaviour
//      KEPT (quality over frequency); the entryMode and minTier tooltips now
//      state the interaction: legacy producers trade only with minTier = 3.
//  C3/C5  Two comments said the opposite of what the code does (the MODULE 11
//      wFired latch IS one-candidate-per-watch by design; pool registration
//      order does NOT prevent same-bar raids — pivot construction does).
//  C4  The tpClamp tooltip claimed all three rungs are capped in every mode;
//      the clamp exists in Liquidity-ladder mode only. Tooltip now says so.
//  Known-and-left in Phase C: poiFresh stays frozen at the tap (5 soft points
//  only) · a second, deeper wick into an already-raided pool does not upgrade
//  its raid grade (raid identity is the FIRST raid, by design) · an HTF POI
//  has no dedupe id (the pool and MSS dedupes still bind FEM re-entries).
//
// ── v5.4 · PHASE D — RUNTIME (TradingView "heavy script" warning) ──────────
//  Pure performance. NO signal, gate, grade, input, alert or drawing changes —
//  every value the engine trades on is bit-identical to v5.3.
//  D1  ZONE GEOMETRY IS CACHED. box.get_top / box.get_bottom are drawing-API
//      reads and this script called them from every per-bar loop: the zone
//      update loop, the next-POI scan, the machine's POI selection, f_nextZone,
//      f_oppLiq and four overlap scans — dozens of API reads per bar. A zone's
//      box coordinates never change after creation (verified: no box.set_top /
//      box.set_bottom exists in the file; flips restyle, they do not move), so
//      Zone now carries `top` / `bot` fields stamped once in f_newZone and the
//      hot paths read those. Display code still uses the box.
//  D2  THE RISK STACK NO LONGER SCANS ON EVERY BAR. f_tp (→ f_nextPool ×2,
//      f_nextZone, f_oppLiq) plus the standalone f_oppLiq room check ran
//      unconditionally for BOTH directions on EVERY bar — ~12 full pool+zone
//      scans per bar to price signals that exist on almost none of them. The
//      scans are gated on `prodX > 0 or barstate.islast` (the established
//      pattern: the gate is passed INTO the function, never a ternary around
//      the call — Pine evaluates both ternary branches). Correctness: okLong /
//      okShort already require prodX > 0, and the only other consumers of
//      RR / room / TP are the dashboard and debug panel, which draw on the
//      last bar only — where the gate is true.
//  What Phase D cannot fix: the 9 request.security instances (5 bias TFs +
//  context + PDH/PDL + HTF CRT/TBS + HTF POI) are a fixed cost — Pine forbids
//  conditional security calls. If the warning persists, the ranked removal
//  levers in AUDIT-v5.md §9 apply (price-action producers → trendlines →
//  verbose debug → order-flow zigzag → HTF CRT/TBS, which also drops one
//  security call), and chart-side: disable the MTF dashboard rows you do not
//  use is NOT possible per-row — but showOF, showStruct chips and showDev off
//  reduce drawing churn.
//
// ── v5.5 · PHASE E — EVENT IDENTITY, THE FOUR MISSING ENGINES, AND 9 DEFECTS ─
// A forensic re-audit of v5.4 (AUDIT-v5.md §14) found nine defects that survived
// Phases A–D and six subsystems the operator brief specifies that this lineage
// had never built. Both are addressed here. Two legacy producers were REMOVED to
// pay for it, with reasons (§40 requires the reasons, not the silence).
//
// ── the nine defects ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  E1  THE RAID CANDLE COULD BE ITS OWN REACTION. f_poolScan's PS_RAID →
//      PS_REACT test was not compared to raidBar, and that path lifts the raid
//      grade RQ_C → RQ_B. So a shallow poke whose own candle carried an
//      unrelated internal CHoCH / CISD was re-graded B on the spot — and
//      RQ_B is exactly what f_grade needs to reach grade A. Same family as
//      V4-BUG-6/7 (an event confirming itself) and V4-BUG-5 (a global flag
//      paying a specific setup). The reaction must now be a LATER bar.
//      NOTE, and this is deliberate: Setup.liqQual is still FROZEN at the
//      stage-3 bind. It looks like V5.3-C1's frozen POI tier and it must NOT
//      get the same treatment — pool.rq improves off GLOBAL flags, so
//      refreshing it would pipe V4-BUG-5 contamination straight into the grade.
//  E2  THE S/D PRODUCER SCORED THE WRONG ZONE'S FRESHNESS. freshL := nbFresh /
//      freshS := nsFresh read the NEXT buy / sell POI from MODULE 9's scan,
//      which is not necessarily sdZoneL / sdZoneS. Now f_zoneFresh(sdZoneL).
//  E3  CROSS-PRODUCER STOP LEAK. lvlPoiL = not na(poiBotL) ? poiBotL :
//      sdBotLong — but PR_SD already writes poiBotL from sdBotLong, so the
//      fallback could ONLY fire for a producer with no POI at all (CRT / turtle
//      soup / trap), handing f_sl an unrelated zone edge as stop candidate 4.
//      A producer with no POI now simply has no POI stop candidate.
//  E4  THE REASON LINE NAMED THE WRONG EVENT. reasonLong / reasonShort and both
//      entry labels read upS.liqTag / upS.sbWindow for EVERY producer, so five
//      of the seven printed the machine setup's raid — usually blank or stale.
//      Event ownership is resolved once now (evTagL / evIdL / evPxL / sbWinL).
//  E5  documented, not "fixed": a setup OUTLIVES the registry retirement of its
//      own bound pool. Only spent / brkBar invalidate; the time- and
//      distance-based retirements are silent, and with reactWin = 6 against the
//      machine's own 12 + 10 + 2 + 25 the pool is routinely dead before the
//      entry. Killing the setup there would kill nearly every setup, because
//      the two clocks are deliberately different — so the DAMAGE is fixed
//      instead, in E7.
//  E6  EIGHT WRITE-ONLY UDT FIELDS removed: Setup.tgtPx / tgtKind / liqPx /
//      liqDepth / dispExt / retestPen, Pool.reactBar, Zone.mitig. Zone.mitig
//      was also COMPUTED every confirmed bar and never read — f_regrade's
//      "50 % mitigated → never better than T2" runs off z.state, not mitig.
//  E7  DEDUPE BY PRICE, NOT ONLY BY ID. E5's real consequence: a retired pool's
//      level re-registers as a NEW pool with a NEW id, so "one liquidity pool →
//      one execution" silently stopped applying to the same physical level.
//      f_dedupeOk now also refuses a second entry within eqTol·ATR of the last
//      executed level on that side, inside setupLife bars.
//  E8  f_blockReason and f_reason were rebuilt on EVERY historical bar — about
//      twenty str.tostring calls and their concatenations, both sides — while
//      their only consumers are the debug table (last bar) and the entry label.
//      Pine evaluates both ternary branches, so the gate had to go INSIDE, the
//      same pattern Phase D used for the risk scanners.
//  E9  neutralised as a side effect: f_poolPush's B1 upgrade moves q.px, which
//      stranded Setup.tgtPx. That field is gone (E6) and evPx reads a pool that
//      is already past PS_VAL, which the upgrade can never re-price.
//
// ── the six subsystems the brief asked for and v5.4 did not have ────────────
//  E · EVENT IDENTITY + DESCRIPTORS (§7 / §8). ONE physical event = ONE Pool =
//      one id. Pool carries `desc` / `descN`, seeded at the raid with its own
//      grade (WICK / SWEEP / DEEP RAID); every engine that recognises the same
//      raid APPENDS a descriptor via f_evTag. Descriptors are worth ONE capped
//      allowance of at most 6 points, between all of them, in a score block
//      that is structurally unable to reach LIQUIDITY — which is still paid
//      exactly once, from that event's own raid grade, pool class and touch
//      history. A sweep that is also a CRT low raid, a turtle-soup extreme, a
//      TWS wave 2 and a bear trap is ONE event with FOUR descriptors, and it
//      now prints that way on the dashboard, the debug panel and the entry
//      reason instead of reading as four confirmations.
//  E1 · CRT IS A STATE MACHINE (§12). v5.4 tested three candles in one boolean.
//      Now: LC_IDLE → LC_ARMED (the reference candle has CLOSED; hi/lo/mid/size
//      stored) → LC_SWEPT (penetration graded CP_TINY / CP_MEAN / CP_DEEP off
//      the SAME raidMinAtr / raidDeepAtr the pool registry uses) → LC_RECL (a
//      later close back through the MID — a different event from the sweep
//      candle's own close-back-inside) → LC_CONF (a later displacement) ·
//      LC_INVAL when the sweep becomes a break. CP_TINY can reach LC_CONF but
//      is never tradable: §12's one-tick-wick rule, enforced not asserted.
//  E2 · TURTLE SOUP IS A COMPOSER (§13). Same lifecycle, with the reference
//      window, the extreme's minimum age, the penetration grading, a decisive
//      reclaim distance and a USER-CHOSEN confirmation rule (reclaim only /
//      reclaim + displacement / reclaim + CISD) as separate stages.
//  E3 · TWS (§14) — three waves: interaction → a deeper, REJECTED manipulation
//      → reclaim with displacement. §14 calls TWS a context engine, so it can
//      never emit, never writes a stop and never ranks: it attaches a
//      descriptor and nothing else.
//  E4 · LIQUIDITY RUN ENGINE (§11). v5.4 had `runUp`/`runDn`: a weak close
//      through ONE level. Now pool A broken → pool B broken = RN_CONT, with an
//      internal → external flag, RN_EXH when the run RAIDS the next pool
//      instead of breaking it, and RN_REV only after exhaustion AND an opposing
//      shift WITH displacement. This carries §11's hard rule: fading a run that
//      is still RN_CONT costs a grade, or is blocked.
//  E5 · CONGESTION FILTER (§29), from price action only (§39 forbids the
//      oscillator): compressed range · most bars overlapping the previous bar ·
//      almost no net travel for the distance covered · structure broken in BOTH
//      directions. Two of four = congested → one grade down, or blocked.
//  E6 · TRAP ENGINE (§15 / §16). v5.4's ① watch, promoted: the broken level must
//      be TRACKED liquidity (a registry pool actually broke — §16's "crowded,
//      obvious liquidity" is what separates a trap from a plain failed
//      breakout), and the trap is GRADED A/B/C from obvious liquidity,
//      breakout depth, reclaim speed, reclaim decisiveness and displacement.
//      A graded trap sets the producer's POI tier; it pays NO liquidity points.
//
// ── removed, with reasons (§40) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  R1  THE DYNAMIC TRENDLINE ENGINE (v5.4 MODULE 10 + PR_TL). Reasons: it is
//      not liquidity or PD-array logic; V5.3-C2 already established that it
//      carries no POI, therefore tier 3, therefore it CANNOT emit at the
//      default minTier = 2 — it was display-only in every shipped
//      configuration; and it is AUDIT-v5.md §9's own second removal lever. Its
//      two plotchars, its [LIQ] alert and its [SIGNAL] alert go with it.
//  R2  PRICE-ACTION PATTERNS ③ INSIDE BAR and ④ DOJI REVERSAL. Reasons: neither
//      contains any liquidity content — they are shape matching, which §39
//      rules out; both were tier 3 and equally unable to emit at defaults; and
//      ④ carried four rolling built-ins (math.sum, ta.sma, ta.highest,
//      ta.lowest) for a producer that could never fire. Their two [SIGNAL]
//      alerts go with them. ① and ② are KEPT — ① became the trap engine above,
//      and ② (shrinking wicks = absorption, a continuation) is kept for
//      backward compatibility and stays the weakest producer in the file.
//
// Kept intact: the machine, FEM, S/D retest, the registry, zones, the FVG
// lifecycle, breaker / mitigation flips, rejection blocks, EQH/EQL, dealing
// range + OTE, killzones, Silver Bullet, volatility windows, CISD, IFC, HTF
// POI, order-flow halves, session liquidity, opening prices, the dashboard, the
// TP ladder, and every remaining alert NAME (including ① and ②).
// Budget after Phase E: 57 / 64 outputs (was 58), 9 request.security instances
// (unchanged), token proxy +7 % on v5.4 — the removals paid for most of the
// build, and no new loop or security call was added.
//
// Every v4 feature is preserved except R1 / R2 above: zones, FVG lifecycle,
// rejection blocks, breaker / mitigation flips, EQH/EQL, dealing range + OTE,
// killzones, Silver Bullet, scheduled volatility windows, trendlines, CRT,
// turtle soup, CISD, IFC, HTF POI, FEM, order-flow halves, session liquidity,
// opening prices, the MTF dashboard, the TP ladder and all 37 alerts.
//
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v5.6 · LIQUIDITY-ONLY FORENSIC UPGRADE
//  A liquidity DETECTION + MARKING pass. Nothing outside the liquidity layer
//  was edited: not the setup machine, not the S/D or FVG lifecycles, not the
//  order / breaker / mitigation blocks, not CISD, not BOS / CHoCH / MSS, not
//  OTE, not the TP / SL ladder, not the sessions, not the HTF engine, not the
//  score, the grades, the gates, the dedupe, the dashboard or a single alert.
//
//  WHAT WAS ALREADY RIGHT (and was left alone). v5.5 already had a real pool
//  REGISTRY with a lifecycle (CREATED → VALIDATED → RAIDED → REACTION → DONE),
//  stable pool identity, raid-vs-break separation, raid grading A/B/C, one
//  event = one pool + descriptors, non-repainting pivot construction, session
//  and PDH/PDL pools, bindable-raid lookup and a liquidity-run engine. It
//  TRACKED liquidity well. It MARKED it barely at all.
//
//  L1  NO NOMENCLATURE ON THE CHART. A pool was an anonymous line: no BSL/SSL,
//      no level name, no strength, no internal/external, no lifecycle text.
//      The only two liquidity labels in the file were the "target BSL / SSL"
//      pair drawn on the last bar. → MODULE 8B, display-only.
//  L2  EQUALS ONLY EVER COMPARED THE IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING PIVOT. One slot per
//      side (lastPhVal / lastPlVal), so a triple top with an offset middle leg,
//      or a level re-tested two pivots later, produced NO EQ pool. → ring of
//      eqScanN pivots, each with its own reaction extreme; every matching leg
//      fused into ONE pool at their mean, tagged EQH×3 / EQL×4.
//  L3  NO PREVIOUS-WEEK LIQUIDITY AT ALL. The single most obvious external
//      stop pool on any intraday chart was unmarked. → PWH / PWL, read from
//      the last CLOSED weekly bar.
//  L4  CLUSTERING DESTROYED CLUSTER INFORMATION. f_poolPush refused or merged
//      any duplicate inside eqTol·ATR, so four stacked highs became one pool
//      that did not know it was four. → a confluence MASK on the pool (which
//      level TYPES resolved here) plus a last-bar grouping pass that names the
//      stop pool "BSL CLUSTER ×4" and boxes its price span.
//  L5  NO STRENGTH. touches and kind existed and were never combined, so a
//      lone minor pivot rendered exactly like a PDH sitting on an EQH.
//      → WEAK / MODERATE / STRONG / EXTREME, from kind, confluence, fused
//      legs, touches, stacking, external position and age.
//  L6  INTERNAL vs EXTERNAL WAS A KIND, NOT A POSITION. PK_EXT meant "an
//      extPiv pivot made this", which is a different statement and goes stale
//      the moment price trades away. → classified against the LIVE dealing
//      range, which is why the marking layer sits after MODULE 8.
//  L7  SWEPT LIQUIDITY STILL DREW AS RESTING. f_sesTrack's Asia / London / NY
//      lines are created with extend.right and never touched again, so a
//      session high taken hours ago still extended into the future — the one
//      place the chart contradicted the registry. → the session lines inherit
//      their pool's state (✓ SWEPT / ⇡ BROKEN) without f_sesTrack being edited.
//  L8  NO MARK FOR AN EXTERNAL RAID. Swing sweeps (◆), EQ raids (◈) and
//      session raids (▵▿) were marked; a PDH / PWH / external-swing raid — the
//      top of the priority ladder — was not. → ◇.
//  L9  THE CAP EVICTED THE OLDEST POOL. A previous-day high that had stood
//      untouched all session died before a three-bar-old minor swing.
//      → optional weakest-first eviction (lqPrunePri, default OFF).
//  L10 TWO HARD-CODED NUMBERS IN THE SWEEP TEST. The wick-vs-body rule was
//      close ≤ high − 0.5·range, with no absolute close-back leg, and the
//      equality tolerance was pure ATR with no tick floor for quantised
//      instruments. → raidRejFrac, raidBackAtr, eqTickMin; all defaulted to
//      the v5.5 values.
//
//  L11 THE TOKEN BUDGET. v5.6 first compiled to 103 094 tokens against a limit
//      of 100 256. The cause was not the new code volume: Pine INLINES a
//      user-defined function at every call site before counting, and f_poolPush
//      is a ~690-token body that v5.5 called FOURTEEN times — ~9 700 tokens for
//      one function, with every token added inside it costing fourteen. All
//      registrations now enqueue and the queue drains through ONE call site
//      immediately before the lifecycle scan (same calls, same order, nothing
//      in between reads the registry, so the behaviour is identical). v5.6 now
//      compiles LIGHTER than v5.5 despite everything it adds.
//  L12 THE BUDGET, SECOND PASS — AND A CORRECTION TO L11. At 102 129 tokens
//      the f_setupReset call sites inside f_setupAdvance (19 of them, in a
//      function instantiated twice) were collapsed to one `kill` flag and one
//      reset site. The count moved by THREE tokens. So the L11 theory is
//      wrong: TradingView's "compiled tokens" is not per-call-site inlining,
//      it is close to linear in SOURCE tokens (comments excluded). The L11
//      queue still helped — it removed fourteen 7-argument call sites — but
//      for the reason that they were source, not because f_poolPush was
//      copied. The kill flag is kept (it is the cleaner shape); the real
//      savings here are textual and behaviour-neutral:
//        · MODULE 18 dashboard rows go through one f_dRow helper instead of
//          two fully-spelled table.cell calls per row;
//        · MODULE 15's ten producer-common field writes (rq / kind / touches /
//          rev / mssCh / mssOk / dispBd / mssB / dispB / poolId) are written
//          once per side after the producer chain instead of in each of the
//          four non-machine blocks;
//        · f_poolScan's mirrored buy-side / sell-side branches are one branch
//          driven by a ±1 sign, with the comparisons that could differ by a
//          float ulp kept in their original per-side form;
//        · dead code removed: f_join / f_add (never called), emaTrend /
//          chartBias / the trendLen input (the Doji producer they served was
//          removed in R2), the fvgWin input (the machine uses FVG_OWN_MAX),
//          recentSsl / recentBsl / recentIBosUp / recentIBosDn / sigRisk
//          (write-only aliases).
//
//  DEFAULTS ARE v5.5-IDENTICAL. Everything in group ④a can change what the
//  REGISTRY contains, and the registry feeds the trade engine — so every one of
//  those inputs ships at the value that reproduces v5.5 exactly (eqScanN 1,
//  eqTickMin 0, raidRejFrac 0.5, raidBackAtr 0, lqPrunePri OFF) with ONE
//  exception, called out in its tooltip: showPW registers PWH/PWL as pools, and
//  a new pool kind is a new target and a new bindable raid. Turn showPW off for
//  output identical to v5.5. Group ④b is pure annotation and can never move a
//  signal.
//
//  NON-REPAINTING. No new lookahead. PWH/PWL uses the same construction as
//  PDH/PDL — the [1] is INSIDE the request, so only a COMPLETED weekly bar is
//  ever visible. Every new pool source is registered on a CONFIRMED bar from a
//  pivot that had already been confirmed pivLen bars earlier. The marking layer
//  reads state and draws; it never back-dates a classification.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v5.7 · ICT DETECTION + MARKING, INLINE (MODULE 16B) — DISPLAY-ONLY
//  Scope lock: nothing outside MODULE 16B was edited. No entry, exit, TP/SL,
//  liquidity, S/D, FVG, CRT, TBS, TWS, session, HTF, score, gate, grade, dedupe,
//  dashboard, plot or alert changed. MODULE 16B READS upS/dnS, zones and the
//  registry; it WRITES only labels and its own UDT.
//
//  WHY IT IS SMALL. v5.6 compiles at ~98 500 of TradingView's 100 256 compiled
//  tokens (measured 2026-09-09) and sits at the CE10295 global-scope cap. The
//  full ICT layer (~16 200 tokens) lives in 10.ict-forensic-layer-v1.pine as a
//  companion overlay. This inline module is the subset that fits the ~1 750
//  token headroom, chosen for ICT value per token:
//    ICT MSS ↑/↓   the machine's OWNED post-raid shift (state 4 → 5), i.e. a
//                  structure shift that happened AFTER a graded raid of the
//                  locked target — not any CHoCH. Chronology already enforced.
//    ICT SEQ       raid → MSS → displacement → FVG completed (state 7 lock),
//                  tagged with OTE when the lock candle sits in the range OTE.
//    ICT IFVG      an FVG zone that was closed through and flipped (MODULE 9's
//                  BREAKER / MITIGATION flip) — the inversion FVG, named as such.
//                  Needs showFlip = ON (a flipped-off zone is deleted instead).
//    ICT JUDAS     London / NY open: a registry-graded raid of a SESSION pool
//                  within ictJWin bars of the open, then a shift or displacement
//                  closing back through the raided level within ictJWin bars.
//    ICT AMD · PO3 the Judas, then a close through the OPPOSITE extreme of the
//                  raided session's range (accumulation → manipulation →
//                  distribution) within 4 × ictJWin bars of the raid.
//  Not included inline (budget): order block, BPR, raid-leg OTE, named raid
//  labels, displacement marks, PD-array-at-price — all in file 10.
//
//  NON-REPAINTING: every write is inside `confirmed`; every input it reads is
//  itself confirmed-bar state. No new request.security. No back-dating.
//  OBJECTS: labels only, created once per event on the confirming bar; they
//  rely on the same 500-label GC every existing chip uses. IFVG ids are held
//  in a 40-slot ring so no zone is tagged twice.
//  COMPILE RISK, stated plainly: this cannot be compiled locally. If TradingView
//  returns CE10117, delete the blocks marked ICT-CUT-1, then -2, then -3, in
//  that order — each is self-contained.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v5.8 · CRT / TBS / TWS FORENSIC MARKING (V5.8-CTT) — DISPLAY-ONLY
//  Scope lock: only CRT / TBS / TWS MARKING changed. The three V5.5-E state
//  machines (f_crtAdvance, f_tbsAdvance, f_twsAdvance), every cand* / SL /
//  score / gate / alert consumer, the HTF CRT/TBS producer and f_evTag are
//  byte-identical to v5.7. What changed:
//    CTT-1  TWS was confirmed (twsConfL/S) but NEVER drawn — only an event tag
//           and an alert. It is marked now: "TWS BUY" / "TWS SELL".
//    CTT-2  The "CRT tiny" / "tsoup tiny" chips printed a one-tick poke as a
//           pattern. CP_TINY no longer earns a CRT / TBS mark or a sweep chip;
//           it stays an event DESCRIPTOR (f_evTag), which is what §12 asks.
//    CTT-3  Vocabulary: "CRT BULL / CRT BEAR", "TBS BUY / TBS SELL",
//           "TWS BUY / TWS SELL", plus a quality tag (MEDIUM / HIGH / VERY HIGH).
//    CTT-4  Confluence: engines confirming on the same side within cttWin bars
//           share ONE label that is UPDATED to "CRT + TBS", "CRT + TWS",
//           "TBS + TWS" or "CRT + TBS + TWS" — no stacked chips, no duplicates.
//    CTT-5  The CRT reference range is drawn ONCE on confirmation as
//           CRT HIGH / CRT EQ / CRT LOW from the stored reference candle; it is
//           frozen at the sweep so it never moves. Capped at cttMaxRng sets,
//           oldest deleted first (own arrays, not the 500-object GC).
//  NON-REPAINTING: all reads are confirmed-bar lifecycle state, all writes are
//  inside `confirmed`. No new request.security. No back-dating of labels.
//  COMPILE: with MODULE 16B present TradingView returned CE10117 — 101 135
//  compiled tokens against the 100 256 cap (879 over; the prediction was
//  ~1 400). Step 1 of the cut order was applied: MODULE 16B (ICT-CUT-1/2/3,
//  ~1 500 tokens) is removed — the full ICT layer runs standalone as
//  10.ict-forensic-layer-v1.pine. Expected now ≈ 99 600. If it STILL fails:
//    2. delete CTT-CUT-1 (CRT HIGH / EQ / LOW lines, ~550);
//    3. delete CTT-CUT-2 (the whole marker) and restore the three v5.7 chips
//       from file 9 lines 3283-3294.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v5.9 · UI READABILITY (V5.9-UI) — TEXT LEGIBILITY ONLY, NOTHING ELSE
//  Reported defect: the on-chart annotations were unreadable. Three separate
//  causes, all of them in the DRAWING layer. No detection, state machine,
//  gate, grade, score, alert, input semantic or calculation was touched — the
//  only inputs that changed are two display DEFAULTS, named below.
//
//   UI-1  TEXT WAS HARD-CODED TO size.tiny IN 20 PLACES. uiInfoSize and
//         uiMicroBig existed and controlled almost nothing: every box caption
//         (zones, breaker / mitigation, rejection blocks, HTF POI, the locked
//         FVG, the premium / discount halves) and every level label (00:00 /
//         08:30 opens, session H / L, CRT HIGH / EQ / LOW) ignored both and
//         printed at size.tiny. Labels now resolve from infoSize / mkSize, so
//         the two existing inputs finally reach them; box captions move to a
//         const size.small (box.new's text_size qualifier is not documented as
//         series, so a variable there is a compile risk that is not worth
//         taking for one step of size). plotchar glyphs keep size.tiny — that
//         argument IS const-only.
//   UI-2  LOW-CONTRAST TEXT. The worst was CRT HIGH / EQ / LOW: the label was
//         drawn with color.new(col, 100) — a fully TRANSPARENT pill — and
//         coloured text, i.e. red or green letters directly on the candles.
//         It now has a solid pill and white text, which is what every other
//         chip in the file already does. Same class of fix, smaller degree:
//         the "crt sweep" chip (bg 72 → 40), the iCHoCH / ibos chips (60 →
//         35), the run chip (70 → 45), the session and opening-price labels
//         (80 / 85 → 45), the zone caption (text 35 → 0), the breaker and
//         mitigation captions (35 → 0), the HTF POI caption (30 → 0), the
//         locked-FVG caption (20 → 0) and the order-flow captions (35 / 65 →
//         10 / 45).
//   UI-3  THREE LABEL COLUMNS WERE STACKED AT ONE X. The liquidity-marking
//         labels, the target BSL / SSL labels, the NEXT BUY / SELL labels and
//         the OTE label all anchored at bar_index + TRADE_BARS, so at any
//         price where two of them existed the text overlapped and neither was
//         readable. They are now four columns: NEXT BUY / SELL and the trade
//         ladder stay at +TRADE_BARS, the liquidity marking layer moves to
//         +2 × TRADE_BARS, target BSL / SSL to +3 × TRADE_BARS, and the OTE
//         label to +3 × TRADE_BARS as well (its box is unmoved).
//   UI-4  LABEL TEXT WAS LONGER THAN IT NEEDED TO BE, which is the other half
//         of an overlap problem. Shortened, with no information removed from
//         the tooltips: the CTT quality word MEDIUM / HIGH / VERY HIGH becomes
//         ★ / ★★ / ★★★ and its "·loc✗" suffix becomes "✗"; the pool lifecycle
//         words become NEW / REST / NEAR / SWEPT / SWEPT✓REV / BROKEN / OLD;
//         "◎ locked FVG · await retest" becomes "◎ FVG · retest"; "HTF 60 FVG
//         · demand POI" becomes "HTF 60 demand POI"; "DISCOUNT · buy flow"
//         becomes "DISCOUNT"; "OTE 62–79% (buy)" becomes "OTE buy"; the
//         "crt sweep" chip becomes "CRT SWP".
//   UI-5  TWO DISPLAY DEFAULTS. cttMaxRng 6 → 3 (six frozen CRT ranges is
//         eighteen lines and eighteen labels on one chart). Nothing else in
//         group ⑩ or anywhere else changed its default, and no input was
//         added or removed.
//
//  TOKEN BUDGET. v5.8 measured ~99 600 of the 100 256 compiled-token cap, so a
//  readability pass had to be token-neutral or negative. It is negative: ten
//  `size.tiny` (3 tokens) became a one-token identifier, four
//  `color.new(color.white, mkTrans)` became `color.white`, and the only
//  additions are three `2 *` / `3 *` multipliers. Comments are not counted.
//  Net ≈ −30 source tokens. Compile still NOT verifiable locally.
//  SCOPE: no line outside a label.new / box.new / f_chip argument list, two
//  text-building expressions and one input default was edited.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.0 · UI READABILITY 2 (V6.0-UI2) — CHIP COLLISION ONLY, NOTHING ELSE
//  Reported defect (screenshot): v5.9 made the text big enough to read but
//  chips still LAND ON TOP OF EACH OTHER, so two marks on one candle print
//  as one unreadable smear. Cause: every chip anchored with yloc.abovebar /
//  yloc.belowbar, which is ONE fixed y per bar per side. CISD, IFC, BOS /
//  CHoCH, iCHoCH / ibos, run, trap, CRT SWP, the CTT marker, the developing
//  preview and the LONG / SHORT entry label all share that anchor, so any
//  two of them firing on the same bar and the same side drew at the same
//  point.
//
//   UI2-1  CHIP LANES. f_chip now positions on PRICE, not on the bar
//          anchor, and keeps a per-bar counter per side (a global int array
//          — a Pine function may mutate an array but never assign a global
//          scalar). The nth chip of a bar sits at high + ATR × (0.45 +
//          n × gap) above, low − ATR × (0.45 + n × gap) below, so marks
//          stack outward in call order instead of overlapping. `var` state
//          is rolled back on every realtime tick, so the counter cannot
//          creep intrabar. Because f_chip needs ATR it is declared in
//          MODULE 3 after `atr` instead of in the rendering preamble; its
//          first call site is far below that.
//   UI2-2  THE ENTRY LABEL AND THE DEVELOPING PREVIEW JOIN THE LANES. The
//          LONG / SHORT label and the ⋯ preview used their own label.new
//          with the same yloc anchor — the exact collision above, and the
//          two marks that matter most. They now route through f_chip,
//          unchanged in colour, transparency and size. Token-NEGATIVE.
//   UI2-3  PREMIUM / DISCOUNT CAPTIONS. Both were text.align_left, i.e.
//          pinned at the left edge of the dealing range, which is the
//          middle of the candles and exactly where the chips live. Both
//          move to text.align_right, into the projection space at the
//          box's right edge. Nothing else about the boxes changed.
//   UI2-4  ONE NEW INPUT. ⑩ Dashboard / UI → "Mark spacing (× ATR)",
//          default 0.9, range 0.2-3.0. Raise it if the chart is zoomed out
//          and stacked chips still touch; lower it on a tall chart.
//
//  NOT CHANGED: detection, every state machine, the CTT mask, gates, score,
//  grades, the trade engine, TP / SL, risk, dedupe, sessions, MTF security
//  calls, the dashboard, the debug panel, alerts and every other input.
//  TOKEN BUDGET: the lane array and its arithmetic cost ~+45 source tokens
//  and the new input ~+18; routing the entry label and the preview through
//  f_chip refunds ~−40. Net ≈ +25 source ≈ +70 compiled against v5.9's
//  ~700 of headroom. Compile still NOT verifiable locally.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.1 · CRT / TBS / TWS ON 15M / 30M / 1H / 4H (V6.1-CTT-MTF) — MARKING ONLY
//  Scope lock: only CRT / TBS / TWS code was added, and NOTHING existing was
//  edited except three lines of it — the indicator title, six new inputs in
//  group ⓪, and the new block that sits after CTT-CUT-2. The v5.5-E state
//  machines (f_crtAdvance / f_tbsAdvance / f_twsAdvance), every cand* / SL /
//  score / gate / alert consumer, the HTF CRT/TBS producer, f_evTag and the
//  whole v5.8-CTT chart-timeframe marker are byte-identical to v6.0.
//
//  WHAT WAS MISSING. CRT, TBS and TWS were CHART-TIMEFRAME engines. The only
//  higher-timeframe path in the file, f_crtTbsHtf, is a single stateless
//  boolean per HTF bar on ONE timeframe (htfTf) with no lifecycle, no reclaim
//  stage, no penetration grade and no TWS at all — and it feeds the trade
//  engine, so it is untouched here. There was no 15M / 30M / 1H / 4H
//  detection, no source-timeframe tag on any structure, and no MTF
//  confluence. MODULE 11C adds exactly that, and marks it.
//
//   MTF-1  ONE MACHINE, FOUR TIMEFRAME CONTEXTS. `f_ctt_htf` is requested on
//          15 / 30 / 60 / 240 and returns fourteen values — the CLOSED HTF
//          candle, the candle before it, that timeframe's ATR, its
//          displacement verdict, its aged TBS extremes and ages, its TWS
//          window extremes, and the HTF bar's `time`. The state machines then
//          run CHART-SIDE inside one `for` loop over the four timeframes and
//          both directions, so the machine code is compiled ONCE instead of
//          twenty-four times. That is the only reason this fits in a file
//          that was already ~99 600 of the 100 256 compiled-token cap.
//   MTF-2  CRT AND TBS SHARE ONE MACHINE (`f_ctt_mach`), because after arming
//          they share every stage. kind 0 = CRT: reference is the PREVIOUS
//          CLOSED candle, it must span ≥ crtRangeAtr × ATR, the sweep must
//          close back INSIDE the range, and the reclaim is the range MID.
//          kind 1 = TBS: reference is an AGED rolling extreme (≥ tbsAgeMin
//          bars old — an extreme two bars old is the current leg, not resting
//          liquidity), the sweep must close back on the origin side, and the
//          reclaim must be DECISIVE (NEAR_LVL_ATR × ATR beyond the level).
//          Both then need a displacement candle on a LATER bar, except the
//          existing 'Close back inside' turtle-soup mode, which confirms at
//          the reclaim exactly as f_tbsAdvance does.
//   MTF-3  TWS KEEPS ITS OWN SHAPE (`f_ctt_tws2`): wave 1 interaction with the
//          window extreme → wave 2 must exceed wave 1 by ≥ twsDeepen × ATR AND
//          close back beyond wave 1 (rejected, not accepted) → wave 3
//          reclaims wave 1 WITH displacement. Acceptance beyond wave 2
//          invalidates — that is a trend, not a manipulation.
//   MTF-4  GRADING AND INVALIDATION are the file's own: penetration graded
//          CP_TINY / CP_MEAN / CP_DEEP off raidMinAtr / raidDeepAtr, printed
//          ★ / ★★ / ★★★, and the minimum quality to MARK defaults to VALID, so
//          a one-tick poke is tracked and not drawn. A close beyond the swept
//          extreme, or crtLife / twsWaveWin bars of that timeframe, retires
//          the structure (LC_INVAL) and it can never confirm afterwards.
//   MTF-5  CONFLUENCE. Each engine passes or fails on its OWN. Inside cttWin
//          chart bars, on ONE timeframe and ONE side, the label is UPDATED to
//          CRT+TBS / CRT+TWS / TBS+TWS / CRT+TBS+TWS instead of stacking a
//          second mark. Three marks landing near each other is never a master
//          confluence: the mask only ever carries independently CONFIRMED
//          legs of the same timeframe.
//   MTF-6  NO DUPLICATES, NO REPAINTING. Each timeframe is only processed when
//          its published `time` CHANGES, and only on a CONFIRMED chart bar, so
//          one structure is drawn once and never re-drawn on a tick. Every
//          value the layer reads is indexed [1] or [2] INSIDE the request, so
//          only CLOSED HTF candles are ever visible; the calls use
//          barmerge.lookahead_off. A timeframe at or below the chart is never
//          requested (the request falls back to the chart timeframe) and never
//          drawn — the v5.8-CTT marker already owns the chart timeframe, so
//          15M can never print as 30M, 1H as 4H, or a chart-TF mark twice.
//   MTF-7  NO SIGNAL. This layer writes labels and lines only. It never feeds
//          cand*, f_sl, f_tp, f_score, f_grade, a gate, the dedupe, a state
//          machine or an alert, and it emits no LONG / SHORT / entry / exit /
//          TP / SL / RR.
//
//  OBJECTS: labels in one FIFO array capped by the new 'MTF structure labels
//  kept' input (default 30); CRT HIGH / EQ / LOW reuse the EXISTING f_cttLvl
//  and therefore the existing 3 × cttMaxRng budget — no new line array, no new
//  object class. 24 UDT instances, allocated once, never re-created.
//
//  TOKEN BUDGET — STATED PLAINLY. v6.0 sat at roughly 600 compiled tokens of
//  headroom. MODULE 11C is far larger than that: the loop architecture is the
//  cheapest correct shape available (machines compiled once, one small feed
//  function × 4, six new inputs, no new constants — LC_*, CP_*, NEAR_LVL_ATR,
//  STRONG_CLOSE_PCT, crtRangeAtr, crtDispAtr, crtLife, tbsLen, tbsAgeMin,
//  tbsConfMode, twsWaveWin, twsDeepen, raidMinAtr, raidDeepAtr, cttWin,
//  cttShowRng, cttMaxRng, atrLen, infoSize and f_cttLvl are all REUSED), but
//  it will very likely return CE10117. If it does, apply these in order —
//  every one of them is inside CRT / TBS / TWS scope, so nothing else moves:
//    MTF-CUT-1  delete the `if okc and cttShowRng` block (the three MTF CRT
//               range levels).
//    MTF-CUT-2  delete `f_ctt_tws2`, its call and okw (TWS keeps its chart-TF
//               engine and its event descriptor; only the MTF TWS mark goes).
//    MTF-CUT-3  delete the 4H context: the ctt_f24 / ctt_e24 lines, the fourth
//               request.security, and change every array.from to three
//               elements and the loop to `for i = 0 to 2`.
//    MTF-CUT-4  delete the whole v5.8-CTT chart-timeframe marker (CTT-CUT-2,
//               ~900 compiled tokens) and let MODULE 11C own the chart
//               timeframe as well by changing the four `ctt_sec <` gates to
//               `ctt_sec <=`.
//    LAST      run 15.crt-tbs-tws-mtf-layer-v1.pine instead — the same layer
//               as a standalone overlay, which always fits.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.2 · CE10295 FIX — MAIN-BODY LENGTH, NOTHING ELSE REMOVED
//  TradingView returned CE10295 on v6.1: "The main body of the script is too
//  long. Try wrapping code in functions." That is NOT the token cap — v6.1's
//  predicted CE10117 never happened, so the compiled-token count is fine and
//  the v6.1 token-budget warning above was wrong. The real limit is how many
//  statements may sit in the script's MAIN BODY (global scope), and this file
//  was already at that limit before MODULE 11C added four tuple destructures,
//  sixteen array.from statements and a nested two-level loop to it.
//
//  NOT ONE FEATURE WAS REMOVED. Every engine, gate, grade, alert, plot, input
//  and drawing of v6.1 is present and unchanged, MODULE 11C included — all
//  four timeframes, all three engines, both directions, the confluence label
//  and the CRT range levels. The only change is WHERE the code lives:
//
//   SC-1  MODULE 11C's main-body footprint goes from ~90 statements to SIX.
//         `f_ctt_feed` now owns one timeframe's request.security AND its
//         destructure, and writes the fourteen values into packed arrays;
//         `f_ctt_proc` owns the whole `if confirmed` loop, both machines,
//         the marking and the range levels. The main body is left with one
//         ATR-seconds line, one init call, four feed calls and one proc call.
//         request.security inside a user function is the file's own existing
//         pattern (f_bias does exactly this), and the timeframe argument
//         stays `simple`, so nothing about the request changed.
//   SC-2  THE STATE IS PACKED, so the declarations cost less main body too:
//         nine arrays become three — ctt_f (10 floats per timeframe), ctt_i
//         (5 ints per timeframe) and ctt_s (6 marking slots per timeframe) —
//         plus the 24 machine instances and the two label arrays. Index
//         arithmetic only: i * 10 + k, i * 5 + k, i * 6 + k, all bounded by
//         i ≤ 3, so no index can leave its array.
//   SC-3  THE v5.8-CTT CHART-TIMEFRAME MARKER moves into `f_ctt_chart`, one
//         call instead of nineteen main-body statements. Identical logic and
//         identical objects; the four cttCrtL / cttCrtS / cttTbsL / cttTbsS
//         flags were read nowhere else in the file, so they are now locals
//         of that function. This is CRT / TBS / TWS code, i.e. in scope, and
//         it hands the main body back MORE statements than MODULE 11C takes.
//
//  NET MAIN BODY: v6.2 is slightly SHORTER than v6.0, which compiled. Nothing
//  outside CRT / TBS / TWS was touched to achieve it — no system was deleted,
//  no input removed, no default changed, and the v6.1 audit's ranked cut
//  levers (MTF-CUT-1 … 4) remain UNUSED and available.
//  Behaviour is bit-identical to v6.1: same machines, same thresholds, same
//  duplicate guard (per-timeframe HTF `time` + confirmed bar), same
//  lookahead_off, same [1] / [2] reads inside the request, same object caps.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.3 · THE COMPILING BUILD — MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED
//  TradingView has now answered both questions with numbers:
//    v6.1 → CE10295 (main body too long)  → fixed in v6.2 by wrapping code
//                                              in functions. That fix is KEPT.
//    v6.2 → CE10117: "compiled code contains too many tokens: 108 630. The
//                      limit is 100 256."  Over by 8 374.
//
//  THE CALIBRATION THOSE TWO ERRORS GIVE US (write it down, it is the whole
//  budget model for this lineage):
//    · v6.2 source = 46 856 raw tokens (comments excluded) → 108 630 compiled
//    · MODULE 11C (the 15M/30M/1H/4H CRT-TBS-TWS layer) = 3 001 raw tokens
//      of source and ≈ 8 890 COMPILED tokens — its cost is ~2.96 compiled per
//      raw token, far above the file's 2.27 average, because four
//      request.security calls each publishing a 14-value tuple are 56 HTF
//      series, and the feed function is inlined once per call site.
//    · THIS FILE WITHOUT MODULE 11C = 108 630 − 8 890 ≈ 99 740 compiled, i.e.
//      about 516 tokens under the cap — which matches v5.8's own estimate of
//      ~99 600 exactly, so the model is trustworthy.
//
//  THEREFORE: the MTF layer needs 8 890 tokens and the lineage file has 516.
//  That is not an optimisation gap, it is seventeen times the entire
//  remaining budget. No amount of restructuring closes it — the only ways to
//  pay for it inside ONE file are to delete large systems that were never in
//  CRT / TBS / TWS scope (measured: the MTF dashboard 2 679 raw ≈ 6 090
//  compiled, the debug panel 1 618 raw ≈ 3 680, the liquidity marking layer
//  1 740 raw ≈ 3 960). Deleting features to add a feature is not an
//  optimisation either, so this build does neither.
//
//  WHAT v6.3 IS: v6.2 with MODULE 11C and its six inputs removed, and
//  EVERYTHING ELSE KEPT — every engine, gate, grade, score, alert, plot,
//  input, default and drawing of v6.0, plus v6.2's main-body optimisation
//  (SC-1 … SC-4), which is pure structure and costs nothing. So this file is
//  the full original indicator, it compiles, and it has ~500 tokens spare.
//
//  WHERE THE MTF MARKING LIVES: 15.crt-tbs-tws-mtf-layer-v1.pine — the SAME
//  layer as a standalone overlay: all four timeframes, all three engines,
//  both directions, the confluence label, the CRT range levels, the quality
//  grading, the duplicate guard and lookahead_off. Add it to the same chart
//  as this file and nothing is lost: one script owns the trade engine, the
//  other owns the MTF structure marks. That is the only arrangement in which
//  BOTH exist, and it is why files 10 and 12 were built the same way.
//
//  KEPT FROM v6.2 (all of it main-body structure, no behaviour change):
//   SC-3  the v5.8-CTT chart-timeframe marker lives in f_ctt_chart().
//   SC-4  the CRT candidate, the TBS candidate, the HTF CRT/TBS producer, the
//         location filter, the TWS runner and the CRT SWP chip are each one
//         function returning one tuple instead of six loose statements.
//  Every global those folds produce — crtCandLong, crtSlLong, tbsCandShort,
//  hCandCrtLong, hSlLong, locLong, atHtfPoiL, twsConfL … — exists with the
//  same value and the same consumers in MODULE 15.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.4 · CRT / TBS / TWS MARKING MOVES TO THE AUTO HTF (V6.4-CTT-HTF)
//  DISPLAY-ONLY. Nothing about the trade engine, the state machines, the
//  producers, the gates, the grades, the score, the dedupe, the risk ladder or
//  a single alert was edited. Only WHICH confirmations earn a mark changed.
//
//  THE REPORTED DEFECT (screenshot, XAUUSD 5m): the chart was carrying roughly
//  twenty CRT / TBS / TWS marks in one session. Four separate causes, all of
//  them in the marking layer:
//    1 · f_ctt_chart() ran the marks off the CHART timeframe. On 5m the CRT
//        machine re-arms on any closed candle spanning >= crtRangeAtr x ATR,
//        which is every few bars, so it swept and confirmed constantly.
//    2 · f_ctt_swp() drew a SEPARATE "CRT SWP" chip on every sweep, on top of
//        the confirmation mark that followed it. Most of the boxed labels in
//        the screenshot were these.
//    3 · Marks were drawn even when the LOCATION filter failed - that is what
//        the trailing "✗" meant. Those patterns can never trade (crtNeedLoc),
//        so they were pure noise.
//    4 · Every confirmation drew CRT HIGH / CRT EQ / CRT LOW: three lines and
//        three labels each, three sets kept.
//
//  THE FIX. The marking layer now reads the SAME auto-HTF map the rest of the
//  file already uses (f_autoHtf -> htfTf): 1m -> 15m · 2m/3m -> 30m · 5m -> 1H ·
//  10m -> 2H · 15m/30m -> 4H · 1H/2H/4H -> D. On a 5m chart you see 1H CRT / TBS
//  and nothing else - a handful of marks a day instead of twenty an hour.
//   H1  NEW INPUT "⑫⑬ CRT / TBS / TWS marking timeframe" - HTF (auto) is the
//       DEFAULT · Chart is the v6.3 behaviour · Both draws each of them.
//   H2  NEW INPUT "Only mark patterns at a valid LOCATION" (default ON) kills
//       every "✗" mark. The location test is the file's own crtNeedLoc logic
//       (HTF POI / engineered or external pool raid / OTE / PD extreme).
//   H3  REMOVED, with the reason (§40): the per-sweep "CRT SWP" chip. It drew
//       on every graded CRT sweep, IN ADDITION to the confirmation mark that
//       followed it off the same range - six of them in one session in the
//       reported screenshot, none of them a signal or even a finished pattern.
//       Nothing it announced is lost: the sweep still advances the lifecycle,
//       still shows in the dashboard's CRT·TS·TWS row, still attaches its event
//       descriptor, and still produces the confirmation mark when the reclaim
//       and the displacement arrive. Its ~60 tokens paid for H4 / H5 below,
//       which is the only way a token-capped file gains a feature.
//   H4  The HTF marks reuse the EXISTING confluence path (f_cttMark), so a CRT
//       and a turtle soup confirming on the same HTF candle share ONE label
//       reading "CRT + TBS", exactly as the chart-TF marker does. The label
//       carries the source timeframe: "CRT BULL ★★ 60".
//   H5  ZERO new request.security calls and zero new HTF series. The HTF marks
//       are drawn from hCrtB / hCrtS / hTbsB / hTbsS, which f_crtTbsHtf has
//       published since v2 and which only the trade engine was reading. That
//       is why this fits in v6.3's ~500 tokens of headroom.
//  KNOWN AND STATED: there is no HTF TWS engine in this file (MODULE 11C, the
//  one that had it, is 8 890 compiled tokens and was removed in v6.3), so in
//  "HTF (auto)" mode TWS is not marked. Use "Both", or run
//  15.crt-tbs-tws-mtf-layer-v1.pine alongside, which marks TWS on 15/30/60/240.
//  CRT HIGH / EQ / LOW are chart-timeframe drawings and follow the chart-TF
//  marker: they appear in "Chart" and "Both", not in "HTF (auto)".
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  v6.4 · TOKEN FIT (V6.5-O) — PURE OPTIMISATION, NOT ONE FEATURE REMOVED
//  The first v6.4 build returned CE10117: "compiled code contains too many
//  tokens: 100 451. The limit is 100 256." Over by 195. This pass buys the room
//  back WITHOUT deleting a single engine, gate, grade, input, default, alert,
//  plot or drawing. Every value the indicator trades on and every mark it draws
//  is identical to that build.
//
//  THE BUDGET MODEL, NOW CALIBRATED ON THREE REAL COMPILE RESULTS
//  (v6.0 = 99 740 · v6.2 = 108 630 · v6.4 = 100 451, all from TradingView):
//    compiled ≈ 3.250 × raw_source_tokens − 13 704   (comments excluded,
//    dotted names counted as one token). Predicts v6.4 at 100 504 against the
//    measured 100 451 — an error of 53 in 100 000, so it is worth trusting.
//    → ONE raw source token is worth 3.25 compiled tokens.
//
//   O1  DEAD HELPER. f_cpTxt had zero call sites once V6.4-H3 retired the
//       per-sweep chip. Removed.
//   O2  DEAD RETURN VALUE. f_crtAdvance published [conf, swept]; `swept` fed
//       only that same removed chip. It returns `conf` now, and the two call
//       sites are plain assignments instead of tuple destructures.
//   O3  TWO DEAD HTF SERIES. f_htfCtx published four values through its
//       request.security — direction, the HTF swing high, the HTF swing low and
//       the premium/discount position. htfSwHi and htfSwLo were read by NOTHING
//       in the file. The pivots that build them are still computed inside the
//       request (the PD position needs them); only the two published series are
//       gone, which is the most expensive kind of token this file spends.
//   O4  THE PRODUCER CHAIN, WRITTEN ONCE. MODULE 15 spelled the identical
//       five-producer if / else-if ladder out TWICE, mirrored LONG / SHORT,
//       preceded by forty default declarations. It is now one `f_prod` function
//       called twice, its twenty results destructured into the same forty
//       globals with the same types and the same defaults. Same producers, same
//       ranks, same tags, same tiers, same stops, same dedupe ids — the only
//       two strings that genuinely differ per side ("Demand / Supply 50%
//       retest" and "① BEAR / BULL TRAP") are chosen from the `isL` argument.
//   O5  73 NAMED CONSTANTS were declared `var <type> NAME = <literal>`. They are
//       plain `NAME = <literal>` now, which makes them CONST-qualified instead
//       of a persistent variable holding a literal. None of the 73 is ever
//       reassigned (verified mechanically), and const is accepted everywhere
//       simple or series is — so this is a strictly stronger qualifier, and
//       it is what `ta.lowest(low, IFC_LOOK)` and the `for k = 0 to PK_N - 1`
//       loop bound wanted in the first place.
//   O6  THE ZONE FLIP, WRITTEN ONCE. The supply→demand and demand→supply
//       breaker / mitigation blocks were mirror images: the same fourteen field
//       writes and six drawing calls, differing only in the target side, the
//       colour, the ▲/▼ arrow and the caption alignment. `f_zoneFlip(z, toSup,
//       isBrk)` carries all four. Byte-identical captions and colours.
//
//  RESULT: 35 141 → 34 747 raw source tokens (−394), i.e. ≈ −1 280 compiled.
//  Predicted 99 224 of the 100 256 cap — about 1 030 spare, where v6.4 was 195
//  over. Main body 1 809 statements against the 2 034 that last compiled, so
//  CE10295 is not in play either (O4 and O6 each hand the main body statements
//  back rather than taking them).
//  NOT CHANGED: every detection engine, state machine, producer, hard gate,
//  grade, score, dedupe rule, risk ladder, session, HTF read, input, default,
//  dashboard row, debug row, plot, label, box, line and alert.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// Signals evaluate on CLOSED bars. Use "Once per bar close" alerts.
// The alignment score is NOT a win probability and this file makes no claim of
// accuracy or profitability. Educational tool. Backtest before live use.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
indicator("ICT Liquidity + PD Array Trade Engine v7.2", "ICT-TE v7.2", overlay = true,
     max_boxes_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ MODULE 1 · INPUTS
// Grouped per the decision hierarchy: Master → General → Structure →
// Liquidity → Supply/Demand → FVG → HTF → Entry engine → FEM → CRT/TBS →
// Patterns → Trendlines → Sessions → Silver Bullet → Volatility → Range →
// Risk → Dashboard → Debug.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
grpMaster = "① Master"
entryMode = input.string("Narrative + confluence", "Entry mode",
     options = ["Narrative only (A+)", "Narrative + confluence", "All engines (legacy)"],
     group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "Narrative only: the ONLY entries are setups whose state machine completed HTF → liquidity target → raid → POI → MSS → displacement → FVG → OTE retest. Fewest, highest quality.\nNarrative + confluence (default): the machine plus the PD-array 50% retest producer — which must still show a bound raid, a POI with provenance, a trigger and displacement.\nAll engines: adds the trap engine under the same confluence gate. Most signals, lowest average quality.\nV7.0: CRT and turtle soup were removed outright, so this setting no longer has anything pattern-matched behind it.")
showDev  = input.bool(false, "Preview developing setups (dim ⋯ mark, no alert)", group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "Draws a faint ⋯ when a setup is one condition away from entry on the CURRENT unclosed bar. Changes no state, raises no alert, and can disappear before the close — that is the point of showing it dim.")
minTier  = input.int(2, "Minimum POI tier for a trade (1 = A+ only, 3 = any)", minval = 1, maxval = 3, group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "Tier 1 = sweep + displacement + MSS + FVG + HTF alignment (+OTE). Tier 2 = displacement + FVG + structure alignment. Tier 3 = zone/FVG geometry only — VISUAL INFORMATION, never tradable above this setting.\nV5.3-C1: the machine reads the LIVE tier (re-graded every bar), so a POI that decays past this setting kills its setup and a decayed T1 no longer buys an A+.\nV5.3-C2: trendline / price-action producers have no POI and grade tier 3 — they only trade at 3.")
needHtfAlign = input.bool(true, "Require HTF context alignment", group = grpMaster)
minGrade  = input.string("B", "Minimum grade to trade", options = ["A+", "A", "B"], group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "A+ = the complete OWNED narrative + a grade-A raid + a tier-1 POI + the A+ RR floor. A = the narrative with one soft ingredient missing, or a confluence producer with full chronology, at tier ≤ 2 with a grade-B raid. B = a graded raid + the B RR floor + a structural target. Anything below B is NOT a grade and does not trade — v5 opened the ladder at B unconditionally, so this gate did nothing (V5.1-A2). A grade is never bought with score alone (V5-9).")
strictSeq = input.bool(true, "Strict chronological sequencing", group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "ON (default): ctxBar < tgtBar < raidBar \u2264 poiBar < mssBar < dispBar \u2264 fvgBar < retestBar. The two \u2264 pairs are the physically simultaneous ones \u2014 the raid candle can be the POI tap, and the displacement candle is the 3rd candle of its own FVG. OFF relaxes only the two context stages (context\u2192target and target\u2192raid) to same-bar; raid < MSS < displacement < retest stay strict in both settings, because a shift that pre-dates its own sweep is not a shift (V4-BUG-1).")
allowRev  = input.bool(true, "Allow counter-HTF REVERSAL setups", group = grpMaster,
     tooltip = "A setup taken against the closed HTF direction is tagged REVERSAL and must show a grade-A raid, a tier-1 POI, a CHoCH-grade MSS and an HTF premium/discount EXTREME. Off = continuation only (V4-BUG-16).")

grpG = "② General"
pivLen     = input.int(5,     "Pivot length (internal swings)",        minval = 2,  group = grpG)
atrLen     = input.int(14,    "ATR length",                            minval = 1,  group = grpG)
biasLen    = input.int(50,    "Bias EMA length (MTF table)",           minval = 5,  group = grpG)
showLevels = input.bool(true, "Show last swing high/low key levels",   group = grpG)
showSweeps = input.bool(true, "Mark liquidity sweeps (◆)",             group = grpG)
biasFilter = input.string("Off", "Bias filter — block counter-trend signals",
     options = ["Off", "5", "15", "60", "240", "D"], group = grpG)

grpM = "③ Structure"
showStruct = input.bool(true,  "Label BOS / CHoCH", group = grpM)
structFilt = input.bool(false, "Filter signals by structure direction", group = grpM)
extPiv     = input.int(12, "External structure pivot length", minval = 5, group = grpM,
     tooltip = "EXTERNAL = major swings: context, major BOS/CHoCH, protected levels, dealing range. INTERNAL (the pivot length above) = MSS / execution confirmation ONLY. v4 never lets an internal CHoCH satisfy a context requirement (BUG-3).")
showOF     = input.bool(true, "Order-flow zigzag (internal legs)", group = grpM)
structDisp = input.float(0.5, "Structural break body ≥ ATR × (0 = any close)", step = 0.1, minval = 0, group = grpM,
     tooltip = "A BOS / CHoCH only counts with a displacement body. A weak close through a level is classified as a liquidity RUN and does NOT burn the level (BUG-2).")
showProt   = input.bool(true, "Plot protected swing (external structure)", group = grpM,
     tooltip = "The protected low (bull) / high (bear) = the actual originating PIVOT of the leg that produced the last BOS — not a running min/max (BUG-1).")
showRuns   = input.bool(false, "Mark weak close-throughs as liquidity runs (run)", group = grpM)

grpLQ = "④ Liquidity"
showEq     = input.bool(true, "Draw equal highs / lows (EQH / EQL)", group = grpLQ)
eqTol      = input.float(0.15, "Equal level tolerance (ATR ×)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.05, group = grpLQ)
eqMinSep   = input.int(5,  "EQH/EQL minimum bar separation", minval = 2, group = grpLQ,
     tooltip = "Two pivots this many bars apart or less are one double-top, not an engineered pool (BUG-7).")
eqNeedReact = input.bool(true, "EQH/EQL require a reaction at both legs", group = grpLQ)
sweepLook  = input.int(15, "Raid stays bindable for (bars)", minval = 1, group = grpLQ,
     tooltip = "A liquidity raid can seed a setup for this many bars. After that the raid is STALE and no setup may claim it (BUG-10).")
poolLife   = input.int(300, "Pool max age (bars, 0 = never expire)", minval = 0, group = grpLQ)
reactWin   = input.int(6,  "Bars allowed for a reaction after a raid", minval = 1, group = grpLQ)
raidMinAtr = input.float(0.10, "Minimum raid penetration (ATR \u00d7)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, group = grpLQ,
     tooltip = "A wick has to actually take the level. Below this the bar is recorded as a TEST of the pool (which raises the pool's quality) and not as a raid (V4-BUG-10).")
raidDeepAtr = input.float(0.35, "Grade-A raid penetration (ATR \u00d7)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.05, group = grpLQ,
     tooltip = "Raid grading: A = this depth or more, closed back inside, strong rejection candle \u00b7 B = raidMinAtr with rejection \u00b7 C = shallow or weak. Grade is a hard requirement for A+ and a soft score elsewhere.")
poolMinTouch = input.int(0, "Prior reactions a pool needs to become a target", minval = 0, maxval = 5, group = grpLQ,
     tooltip = "0 = any live pool can be hunted. 1+ demands that price has already respected the level, which is what makes it real resting liquidity rather than an arbitrary pivot.")
showPools  = input.bool(true, "Draw the liquidity registry (targets + raids)", group = grpLQ)
poolMaxN   = input.int(28, "Max pools tracked", minval = 6, maxval = 60, group = grpLQ)
showSesHL  = input.bool(true, "Session high / low liquidity lines", group = grpLQ)
showPD     = input.bool(true, "Previous day high / low (PDH / PDL)", group = grpLQ)

// ─── V5.6 · LIQUIDITY DETECTION (feeds the registry, so it can move signals) ──
// Every input in this block is defaulted to the value that makes the registry
// BIT-IDENTICAL to v5.5. Nothing here changes a signal until you change it.
grpLQD = "④a Liquidity · detection (v5.6)"
eqScanN    = input.int(1, "Equal-level pivot memory (pivots compared)", minval = 1, maxval = 6, group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "v5.5 compared a new pivot ONLY with the pivot immediately before it, so a triple top whose middle leg was offset, or an equal high matching two pivots back, was never registered.\n1 = v5.5 behaviour exactly. 2-6 = compare against that many stored pivots; ALL legs that match are fused into ONE pool at their mean, tagged EQH×3 / EQL×4 etc.\nRAISING THIS ADDS POOLS, and pools are targets — this is the one detection setting that can change what the trade engine sees.")
eqTickMin  = input.int(0, "Equal tolerance floor (ticks)", minval = 0, maxval = 500, group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "The equality / dedupe tolerance is max(ATR × tolerance, this × mintick). 0 = v5.5 (pure ATR). Raise it on tick-quantised instruments (indices, some crypto perps) where a dead-flat session collapses ATR and the ATR tolerance becomes narrower than the spread. Suggested: 0 forex · 10-20 XAUUSD · 20-50 indices · 0 crypto.")
raidRejFrac = input.float(0.5, "Sweep rejection: close inside by ≥ this × bar range", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, maxval = 1.0, group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "§11 wick-vs-body validation, made configurable. A raid is only graded 'rejected' when the close sits this far back from the swept extreme, measured on the raid candle's own range. 0.5 = v5.5.")
raidBackAtr = input.float(0.0, "Sweep rejection also needs this much close-back (ATR ×)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "Second, ABSOLUTE leg of the same test: the close must also finish this far back INSIDE the swept level. 0 = v5.5 (relative test only). Use it to kill one-tick pokes on instruments whose bar ranges are erratic.")
lqPrunePri = input.bool(false, "Prune the WEAKEST pool, not the oldest, at the cap", group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "v5.5 evicts array slot 0 (the oldest) when the registry is full, so a PDH from yesterday dies before a 3-bar-old minor swing. ON ranks by kind priority, confluence and touches and evicts the weakest un-raided pool instead. OFF = v5.5 eviction order.")
showPW     = input.bool(true, "Previous week high / low (PWH / PWL)", group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "Registers the completed previous week's extremes as external pools, read from the last CLOSED weekly bar ([1] inside the request), so they never repaint. They then inherit the whole registry for free: raid grading, the sweep / break split, the lifecycle and the marking layer.")
// V7.0 \u00b7 \u00a722 \u00b7 DYNAMIC TRENDLINE LIQUIDITY
showTL     = input.bool(true, "Trendline liquidity (swept sloped highs / lows)", group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "\u00a722. Two CONFIRMED external pivots of the same side define a sloped line. It only becomes LIQUIDITY once price has respected it enough times to have stops resting on it, and it only enters the registry at the moment it is SWEPT \u2014 taken and closed back inside. A trendline break on its own never produces anything, which is the rule the brief states.")
tlMinTouch = input.int(2, "Trendline touches before it is liquidity", minval = 1, maxval = 8, group = grpLQD)
tgtDistW   = input.float(1.5, "Target selection \u2014 distance penalty (per ATR)", step = 0.25, minval = 0.0, group = grpLQD,
     tooltip = "\u00a77. v6.4 always hunted the NEAREST live pool, so a minor swing 3 ticks away outranked the previous day high. Selection is now a RANKING: level type, confluence and prior respect, minus this much per ATR of distance. 0 = pure quality (the furthest PWH can be the target); large = the v6.4 nearest-pool behaviour.")
tgtMaxAtr  = input.float(25.0, "Target must be within (ATR \u00d7)", step = 5.0, minval = 3.0, group = grpLQD)

// ─── V5.6 · LIQUIDITY MARKING (pure display — cannot move a signal) ──────────
grpLQM = "④b Liquidity · marking (v5.6)"
lqMark     = input.bool(true, "Enable the liquidity marking layer", group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "Annotates the pool registry with BSL / SSL, strength, internal vs external, clusters and lifecycle state. Rendering happens on the last bar only — no historical objects, no leaks, no effect on any calculation.")
lqShowBSL  = input.bool(true, "Mark buy-side liquidity (BSL)",  group = grpLQM)
lqShowSSL  = input.bool(true, "Mark sell-side liquidity (SSL)", group = grpLQM)
lqMinStr   = input.string("MODERATE", "Minimum strength to label",
     options = ["WEAK", "MODERATE", "STRONG", "EXTREME"], group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "§21 anti-clutter. The registry still TRACKS everything; this only decides what earns a label. MODERATE hides lone minor pivots.")
lqMaxLbl   = input.int(12, "Max liquidity labels", minval = 2, maxval = 30, group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "§13 priority. When more levels qualify than this, the strongest win: external > previous week > previous day > equal > major swing > cluster > internal > minor.")
lqShowInt  = input.bool(true, "Label INTERNAL liquidity (inside the dealing range)", group = grpLQM)
lqShowExt  = input.bool(true, "Label EXTERNAL liquidity (at / beyond the range)",    group = grpLQM)
lqShowClu  = input.bool(true, "Mark liquidity clusters (pools)", group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "§6/§7. Highs stacked within the cluster radius are ONE stop pool. The strongest member is labelled 'BSL CLUSTER ×n' and a translucent box spans the pool's price range; the other members are silenced.")
lqCluTol   = input.float(0.35, "Cluster radius (ATR ×)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.05, group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "Must be WIDER than the equal-level tolerance: levels closer than that tolerance were already fused into one pool at registration. The gap between the two is where clusters live.")
lqCluMin   = input.int(2, "Minimum levels to call it a cluster", minval = 2, maxval = 6, group = grpLQM)
lqShowSwept = input.bool(true, "Keep swept liquidity marked (✓ SWEPT)", group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "§9. A swept level stops extending and is redrawn as history — it is never shown as if it were still resting.")
lqSweptBars = input.int(20, "Swept liquidity stays labelled for (bars)", minval = 0, maxval = 200, group = grpLQM)
lqSesState = input.bool(true, "Retire the session H/L lines once swept", group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "v5.5 drew Asia/London/NY extremes as lines that extend right FOREVER, even after the level was taken — the one place the indicator still showed swept liquidity as resting. This syncs those lines to their registry pool.")
lqRaidChar = input.bool(true, "Plot external-liquidity raids (◇ PDH/PWH/EXT)", group = grpLQM,
     tooltip = "v5.5 marked swing sweeps (◆), EQ pool raids (◈) and session raids (▵▿) but had no mark at all for a PDH / PDL / PWH / PWL / external-swing raid.")

grpZ = "⑤ Supply / Demand"
showZones  = input.bool(true,   "Draw zones",                          group = grpZ)
dispFactor = input.float(1.2,   "Displacement body ≥ ATR ×", step = 0.1, minval = 0.5, group = grpZ)
maxZoneAtr = input.float(2.0,   "Max zone height (ATR ×)",   step = 0.5, minval = 0.5, group = grpZ)
maxZones   = input.int(8,       "Max zones per side", minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = grpZ,
     tooltip = "Raised from 6 in v7.0: the PD-array ladder now also produces ORDER BLOCKS and tags BPR overlaps, and at 6 those crowded the FVG zones out of the registry.")
showOB     = input.bool(true,   "Order blocks (last opposing candle of a displacement leg)", group = grpZ,
     tooltip = "\u00a714. An OB is only created when the displacement leg that left it ALSO broke structure and left an FVG \u2014 never for every opposite-coloured candle. It carries the same provenance fields as every other zone.")
showBPR    = input.bool(true,   "Tag balanced price ranges (opposing FVG overlap)", group = grpZ,
     tooltip = "\u00a717. When a new FVG overlaps a live FVG of the opposite polarity the overlap is a BPR: the surviving zone is re-tagged and promoted to tier 2. It is a PD array, never a signal of its own.")
needSweep  = input.bool(true,   "S/D producer: the zone must CONTAIN the raid extreme", group = grpZ,
     tooltip = "V7.2 BUG-02. v7.1 handed the confluence producer the most recent raid on its side no matter where that raid was: the zone and the liquidity event it claimed as its reason had no spatial relation at all, only a time ordering of global flags. ON (default) requires the raid wick to sit inside the zone, within the same outside slack the machine uses (POI containment slack). OFF reproduces v7.1.")
showNext   = input.bool(true,   "Highlight NEXT buy / sell zone", group = grpZ)
confirmWin = input.int(5,       "Max bars from 50% tap to confirmation", minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = grpZ)
showFlip   = input.bool(true,   "Flip violated zones into breaker / mitigation blocks", group = grpZ,
     tooltip = "v4 requires a CAUSAL chain on the SAME zone: the zone was created, price returned into it, a liquidity event happened AT it, displacement broke it and structure shifted → BREAKER. Any other failure → MITIGATION. v2 used unrelated recent flags (Phase 12).")
showRB     = input.bool(true,   "Rejection blocks (swing-point wick POIs)", group = grpZ)
showTier   = input.bool(true,   "Show tier tag on zones (T1/T2/T3)", group = grpZ)

grpFV = "⑥ FVG"
showFvgState = input.bool(true, "Annotate FVG lifecycle (fresh / 50% / filled)", group = grpFV)
fvgFillDead  = input.bool(true, "A fully filled FVG stops being a POI", group = grpFV,
     tooltip = "A 50% tap is SETUP PREPARATION, not an entry. A 100% fill means the imbalance is gone — v4 retires it as a POI but keeps the box for context (Phase 10).")

fvgMinAtr    = input.float(0.10, "Minimum FVG height (ATR \u00d7)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, group = grpFV,
     tooltip = "A gap thinner than this is noise: its 50 % is inside the spread on most instruments and a retest of it cannot be measured. Applies to the machine's execution FVG.")

grpH = "⑦ HTF"
htfSel    = input.string("Auto", "HTF for POI / structure / liquidity", options = ["Auto", "15", "30", "60", "240", "D"], group = grpH)
htfCtxSel = input.string("Auto", "HTF for CONTEXT (bias / structure / PD)", options = ["Auto", "15", "30", "60", "240", "D"], group = grpH,
     tooltip = "Separate from the bias FILTER so turning the filter on no longer changes the score (BUG-16). Auto: 1m→15m, 5m→1H, 15m→4H, 30m/1H+→D.")
htfLiqReg = input.bool(true, "Register HTF swing highs / lows as liquidity pools", group = grpH,
     tooltip = "§7 / §25. The HTF's own confirmed swing extremes are the most relevant external liquidity an LTF chart has, and v6.4 tracked none of them. They arrive through the ordinary registry (PK_HTF), so they inherit raid grading, the sweep / break split, the lifecycle, the marking layer and target selection. Read from a CLOSED HTF pivot - the [1] is inside the request.")
showPoi   = input.bool(true, "Draw live HTF FVG POI boxes", group = grpH)
hPoiMaxAge   = input.int(40, "HTF POI max age (HTF candles)", minval = 5, group = grpH)
hPoiMaxTouch = input.int(3,  "HTF POI max candles inside before expiry", minval = 1, group = grpH)
hPoiDisp     = input.float(0.8, "HTF FVG min displacement body (ATR ×)", step = 0.1, minval = 0, group = grpH,
     tooltip = "BUG-17: v2 accepted any 3-candle HTF gap, session-open gaps included.")
hPoiNeedDir  = input.bool(true, "HTF POI must agree with HTF direction", group = grpH)

grpE = "⑧ Entry engine"
setupLife = input.int(60, "Setup max life (bars) before invalidation", minval = 10, group = grpE)
mssWin    = input.int(12, "Max bars: raid→POI, and POI→MSS", minval = 2, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "Used twice: the POI must be confirmed within this many bars of the raid, and the MSS within this many bars of the POI. A reaction that takes longer than this is not a reaction to that raid.")
dispWin   = input.int(10, "Max bars from MSS to displacement", minval = 1, group = grpE)
retestWin = input.int(25, "Max bars from the FVG to its retest", minval = 2, group = grpE)
poiBindAtr = input.float(0.15, "POI must CONTAIN the raid extreme — slack outside the array (ATR ×)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "Causality guard (Phase 7 / BUG-10), TIGHTENED IN V7.1 §7 and re-read as CONTAINMENT. v7.0 shipped this at 2.0 and used it as a PROXIMITY test: any array whose nearest edge fell within two ATR of the raid wick could be bound as the POI — which is a different trade wearing this one's reason. It is now the slack allowed OUTSIDE the array, so 0.15 means the zone effectively has to hold the wick that took the pool. 0.0 = literal containment. Set it back to 2.0 to reproduce v7.0 exactly. Also read by the zone tier grader and by the HTF-array location test, which were proximity tests for the same reason.")
mssNeedChoch = input.bool(false, "MSS must be a CHoCH (reject CISD-only shifts)", group = grpE)
retestDepth = input.float(0.5, "FVG retest depth required (0.5 = 50%)", step = 0.1, minval = 0.0, maxval = 1.0, group = grpE)
lateBars   = input.int(6, "LATE entry \u2014 max bars from the FIRST FVG touch to the entry", minval = 1, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "\u00a748. Price entering the gap starts the clock. A rejection that only arrives many candles later is a LATE entry: the imbalance has been sitting mitigated, the RR has decayed and the move has usually already gone. HARD gate, reported as LATE in the debug panel.")
poiMaxAge  = input.int(80, "Max POI age for binding (bars)", minval = 5, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "V7.1 §40. A zone that has been sitting on the chart for hundreds of bars has been traded through, mitigated and re-priced many times over, whatever its live tier still says. Older than this and it can no longer be bound as a setup's POI.")
dispBodyAtr = input.float(1.3, "Setup displacement body ≥ ATR ×", step = 0.1, minval = 0.5, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "V7.1 §2. The STAGE-6 requirement, separate from the chart-wide \"Displacement body\" that merely marks a candle. Institutional delivery is a range expansion; 1.3 × ATR is the floor at which a candle stops being ordinary. Must be ≥ the chart-wide figure to have any effect.")
apMaxTouch = input.int(2, "A+ only within this many touches of the execution FVG", minval = 1, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "V7.1 §48. Each fresh entry into the gap after having been fully outside it is one more mitigation of the array. The first and second are the unmitigated ones; after that the setup can still trade, but it is capped at grade A.")
minScore = input.int(0, "Min alignment score (0 = off)", minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = grpE,
     tooltip = "Context 25 · Liquidity 25 · POI 20 · Trigger 20 · Execution 10. ALIGNMENT of the narrative — NOT win probability, and never sufficient on its own.")
masterCd = input.int(6, "Cooldown after a final signal, per direction (bars)", minval = 0, group = grpE)
dedupePool = input.bool(true, "One liquidity pool \u2192 one execution", group = grpE,
     tooltip = "Once a pool has produced an entry it can never seed another, in either direction, for as long as it is tracked (V4-BUG-14).")
dedupePoi  = input.bool(true, "One POI \u2192 one execution", group = grpE)
dedupeMss  = input.bool(true, "One MSS \u2192 one execution", group = grpE,
     tooltip = "Blocks the second and third entry that price generates by returning to the same FVG after the same structure shift.")

grpF = "⑨ Fractal entry model (FEM)"
useFEM  = input.bool(true, "◎ Enable FEM (HTF POI is the setup's POI)", group = grpF,
     tooltip = "FEM is no longer a separate chain that can fire on its own. It is the state machine running with the HTF FVG POI as the POI stage, which is why it outranks every other producer (Phase 15 / BUG-18).")
femWait = input.int(40, "Max bars from HTF POI tap to execution", minval = 5, group = grpF)

grpC = "⑩ Delivery · CISD / IFC / inducement"
showCisd   = input.bool(true,  "Label CISD (change in state of delivery)", group = grpC)
cisdAsConf = input.bool(false, "Accept CISD as retest confirmation", group = grpC)
showIFC    = input.bool(true,  "Mark IFC candles (grab + displacement)", group = grpC)
// V7.0-ICT · CRT and TURTLE SOUP ARE GONE. Fifteen inputs, two lifecycle state
// machines, two producers, the HTF CRT/TBS request.security, the whole CTT
// marking layer and five alerts were removed. Nothing below reads them.
useInduce  = input.bool(true, "Inducement — minor liquidity taken before the true raid", group = grpC,
     tooltip = "§38. An INTERNAL (minor swing) pool taken on the SAME side BEFORE this setup's raid is the inducement that pays for the real sweep. It is scored and it is an A+ ingredient; it is never required on its own, because plenty of clean external raids happen without one.")
induceLook = input.int(30, "Inducement stays relevant for (bars)", minval = 5, group = grpC)
mssNeedDisp = input.bool(true, "A CISD may only act as MSS with a displacement leg", group = grpC,
     tooltip = "§5. CISD is a change in the STATE OF DELIVERY, not automatically a market-structure shift. ON keeps CISD usable as the machine's stage-5 shift only when the same candle also displaces; a CHoCH never needs it. OFF is the v6.4 behaviour (any CISD is an MSS).")
grpS = "⑪ Trap engine (failed breakout → reclaim)"
showS1     = input.bool(true,  "① Breakout Rejection Trap (growing wicks)",  group = grpS)
showS2     = input.bool(true,  "② Pin Bar Liquidity Trap (shrinking wicks)", group = grpS)
minWickAtr = input.float(0.3,  "Min significant wick (ATR ×)", step = 0.1, group = grpS)
maxAfterBrk = input.int(8,     "Max bars watched after breakout", minval = 3, group = grpS)
trapNeedPool = input.bool(true, "1 The broken level must be TRACKED liquidity", group = grpS,
     tooltip = "V5.5-E6. A trap is a failed breakout of CROWDED, obvious liquidity. ON = a pool in the registry had to be BROKEN on the arming bar, not merely a pivot violated. OFF reverts to v5.4 behaviour (any structural break arms the watch).")
trapElite  = input.bool(true, "1 An ELITE trap reaches tier 1 and fires in every entry mode", group = grpS,
     tooltip = "V7.1 §15/§16. v7.0 capped every trap at tier 2 and switched the producer off outside \"All engines\", so the one pattern ICT treats as a primary entry model could never reach A or A+. An ELITE trap is all five ingredients at once — tracked liquidity broken, a shallow overshoot, a reclaim inside the bar limit, a decisive close back through the level and a displacement body. That is a complete causal story, so it is allowed a tier-1 POI, is A+ eligible with a grade-A raid behind it, and is admitted in Narrative-only mode. Ordinary and ② traps are unchanged.")
trapReclMax  = input.int(5,  "1 Reclaim within this many bars = grade A", minval = 1, group = grpS,
     tooltip = "V5.5-E6 reclaim speed. One of the five ingredients of trap quality; the others are obvious liquidity, breakout depth, a decisive reclaim close and a displacement body.")

grpW = "⑫ TWS · liquidity run · congestion"
useTws     = input.bool(true, "TWS three-wave sequence (context / descriptor only)", group = grpW,
     tooltip = "V5.5-E3. TWS can never emit a signal, never writes a stop and never ranks above another producer. It attaches a descriptor to the liquidity event and is worth a capped context bonus - which is exactly the role the brief gives it.")
twsWaveWin = input.int(15, "TWS max bars per wave", minval = 3, group = grpW)
twsDeepen  = input.float(0.15, "TWS wave 2 must be deeper by (ATR x)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.0, group = grpW,
     tooltip = "Wave 2 is the MANIPULATION: it must take out wave 1's extreme by at least this much and then close back beyond wave 1. A marginal new extreme is drift, not manipulation.")
runGate    = input.string("Downgrade", "Liquidity-run gate - fading an unexhausted run",
     options = ["Off", "Downgrade", "Block"], group = grpW,
     tooltip = "V5.5-E4. The brief's hard rule: never fade a liquidity run merely because liquidity was taken. While a run is classified RUN (pool A broken, then pool B broken, same direction) a counter-trade is a fade, and it is downgraded one grade, or blocked. Once the run EXHAUSTS (it raided the next pool instead of breaking it) or REVERSES (exhaustion plus an opposing shift with displacement) the gate opens.")
runLife    = input.int(40, "Run stays live for (bars since the last leg)", minval = 5, group = grpW)
congLen    = input.int(10, "Congestion window (bars)", minval = 4, group = grpW)
congGate   = input.string("Downgrade", "Congestion filter",
     options = ["Off", "Downgrade", "Block"], group = grpW,
     tooltip = "V5.5-E5, measured from price action only - no oscillator. Four readings: compressed range, most bars overlapping the previous bar, almost no net travel for the distance covered, and structure broken in BOTH directions inside the window. Two of four = congested, so one grade down, or blocked.")

grpK = "⑬ Sessions / Killzones (New York time)"
useKZ   = input.bool(false, "Only signal inside killzones (intraday charts)", group = grpK)
paintKZ = input.bool(false, "Shade killzones", group = grpK)
asiaSes = input.session("2000-0000", "Asia", group = grpK)
lonSes  = input.session("0200-0500", "London", group = grpK)
nySes   = input.session("0700-1000", "New York AM", group = grpK)
showOpens = input.bool(true, "00:00 midnight + 08:30 open lines", group = grpK)

grpSB = "⑭ Silver Bullet"
sbRole   = input.string("Filter only (recommended)", "Silver Bullet role",
     options = ["Off", "Filter only (recommended)", "Boost score only"], group = grpSB,
     tooltip = "The window is NEVER a signal (Phase 20). Filter only: a window entry must still complete the full narrative and gets the SB tag. Boost score only: the window adds session points and nothing else.")
sbOnlyIn = input.bool(false, "Restrict ALL entries to the Silver Bullet windows", group = grpSB)
sbSesLO  = input.session("0300-0400", "Silver Bullet — London open", group = grpSB)
sbSesAM  = input.session("1000-1100", "Silver Bullet — NY AM", group = grpSB)
sbSesPM  = input.session("1400-1500", "Silver Bullet — NY PM", group = grpSB)

grpN = "⑮ Scheduled volatility windows (NY time) — NOT a live calendar"
useNews   = input.bool(true,  "Block LONG / SHORT signals inside the windows", group = grpN,
     tooltip = "Fixed clock windows around the usual 08:30 / 10:00 / Wednesday 14:00 US release slots. This is a SCHEDULED VOLATILITY FILTER, not an economic calendar — not every 08:30 is CPI/NFP and not every Wednesday is FOMC. Edit the sessions to match the week. Day codes: 1=Sun … 7=Sat.")
paintNews = input.bool(true,  "Shade the windows", group = grpN)
news1Ses  = input.session("0825-0845:23456", "Window #1 — 08:30 US data slot", group = grpN)
news2Ses  = input.session("0955-1010:23456", "Window #2 — 10:00 US data slot", group = grpN)
news3Ses  = input.session("1355-1435:4",     "Window #3 — 14:00 FOMC slot (Wednesdays)", group = grpN)

grpO = "⑯ Dealing range / OTE"
showRange = input.bool(true, "Show range EQ + OTE 62–79% box", group = grpO)
showFlow  = input.bool(true, "Shade order-flow areas (premium / discount)", group = grpO)
rangeSrc  = input.string("External", "Dealing range swings", options = ["External", "Internal"], group = grpO)
oteLegMode = input.bool(true, "OTE from the SETUP'S OWN impulse leg (not the global range)", group = grpO,
     tooltip = "§10 / §11. ON (default): a setup's OTE is measured on the leg IT created - raid extreme to displacement extreme - so the 62 / 70.5 / 79 % retracement belongs to the move being traded. OFF falls back to the chart's dealing range, which is what v6.4 used for every setup regardless of where the impulse came from.")
oteNeedAp = input.bool(true, "A+ requires the entry to sit in its own OTE", group = grpO)

grpR = "⑰ Risk · SL / TP"
showTrade = input.bool(true, "Show entry / SL / TP of the last final signal", group = grpR)
tpMode    = input.string("Liquidity ladder", "Take-profit mode",
     options = ["Liquidity ladder", "Next level", "R multiple"], group = grpR,
     tooltip = "Liquidity ladder: TP1 = internal liquidity · TP2 = the nearest opposing wall / external pool at least the grade-B RR floor away · TP3 = external liquidity, all three clamped short of un-raided opposing POIs.\nRR IS MEASURED TO TP2, so in ladder and Next-level mode a setup with no structural target that far away is BLOCKED (debug: \"no structural target\"). In R multiple mode TP2 is rrMult by definition, which makes the RR gate inert BY REQUEST — the grade ladder then rests on raid quality and POI tier alone.")
rrMult    = input.float(2.0, "Take-profit R multiple (fallback + TP2 floor)", step = 0.5, minval = 0.5, group = grpR)
minRiskAtr = input.float(0.25, "Minimum stop distance (ATR ×)", step = 0.05, minval = 0.05, group = grpR)
maxRiskAtr = input.float(3.0,  "Maximum stop distance (ATR ×)", step = 0.25, minval = 0.5, group = grpR,
     tooltip = "A structural level further than this is rejected and the hierarchy falls through to the next candidate. This is what stops an HTF-sized stop from being applied to a 5m entry.")
atrStopMult = input.float(1.0, "ATR fallback stop (ATR ×)", step = 0.25, minval = 0.25, group = grpR)
tpClamp   = input.bool(true, "Clamp a TP that sits behind an un-raided opposing pool", group = grpR,
     tooltip = "ON (default): ALL THREE rungs are capped just short of the nearest un-raided opposing pool OR opposing POI, whichever is closer. v5 clamped TP3 only, and only against zones. Note the consequence, which is intended: a wall closer than the grade-B RR floor caps TP2 below that floor and the trade is refused for RR. That is a target you have no room to reach, not a trade.\nV5.3-C4 scope: the clamp exists in LIQUIDITY LADDER mode only. In 'Next level' mode TP2 is itself the next structural level and TP3 is aspirational (unclamped); 'R multiple' is synthetic by request.")
rrAplus   = input.float(2.0, "Minimum RR \u2014 A+", step = 0.1, minval = 0.5, group = grpR,
     tooltip = "RR is measured to TP2 of the LIQUIDITY LADDER, which is a real level, never rrMult (V5.1-A1). It is computed BEFORE the signal is emitted. Each grade owns its floor as a PRECONDITION, so a candidate that misses the A+ floor falls to A rather than keeping the A+ tag. The opposing-liquidity room is a SEPARATE gate — v5 folded it into RR with math.min(), which made the documented soft mode hard-block through the wrong gate.")
rrA       = input.float(1.5, "Minimum RR \u2014 A",  step = 0.1, minval = 0.5, group = grpR)
rrB       = input.float(1.5, "Minimum RR \u2014 B",  step = 0.1, minval = 1.5, group = grpR,
     tooltip = "\u00a732 \u2014 THE HARD FLOOR. No grade exists below this and minval is 1.5, so it cannot be configured away. A+ prefers 2.0.")
useOppLiq = input.bool(true, "Opposing-liquidity room check", group = grpR,
     tooltip = "Before entry, measure the distance to the nearest opposing pool or opposing POI. Risk 10 with resistance 8 away is not a 2R trade however the maths is drawn.")
oppLiqMult = input.float(1.0, "Opposing liquidity must be \u2265 this \u00d7 risk away", step = 0.25, minval = 0.0, group = grpR)
oppLiqHard = input.bool(false, "Treat the opposing-liquidity check as a HARD block", group = grpR,
     tooltip = "OFF (default): too little room downgrades the grade by ONE step — A+→A, A→B, and B→no trade, because one step below B is not a trade. ON: it blocks outright at any grade. RR is never altered by this either way (V5.1-A5).")

grpD = "⑱ Dashboard / UI"
showDash = input.bool(true, "Show MTF bias dashboard", group = grpD)
uiSizeOpt  = input.string("Normal", "Signal label size", options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal", "Large", "Huge"], group = grpD,
     tooltip = "Size of the LONG / SHORT entry label. Existing charts keep whatever they were saved with — set it here to change it.")
